{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y4khw4Uw693fbCykEFWqWU","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Method}\n2\t\\label{sec:method}\n3\t\n4\t\\subsection{Style-Controlled Rewriting}\n5\t\\label{sec:rewriting}\n6\t\n7\tWe aim to isolate the effect of individual style attributes on judge scores by generating text pairs that differ along exactly one style axis.\n8\tFor each text, an instruction-tuned LLM (Qwen3-32B) first classifies its current style along the target axis (e.g., formal vs.\\ casual via binary classification), then rewrites it toward the opposite pole (temperature 0.7) according to an axis-specific prompt (e.g., ``Rewrite the following text to be more formal/casual while preserving the exact same content and meaning'').\n9\tThis produces a dataset balanced across both directions at the dataset level: texts detected as formal are rewritten to casual, and vice versa.\n10\tEach operation constitutes a binary intervention on a single style variable while all other axes remain fixed (Section~\\ref{sec:causal_framework}).\n11\tTo control for rewriting artifact bias---the possibility that the judge prefers original text over any rewrite regardless of style---we additionally generate a same-style double-rewrite for each text (e.g., formal $\\to$ formal) to form an original-vs-double-rewrite control pair, following the RATE bias correction protocol \\citep{reber2025rate}.\n12\t\n13\t\\subsection{Content Preservation Verification}\n14\t\\label{sec:nli_gate}\n15\t\n16\tStyle-controlled rewriting risks altering semantic content, which would confound the style effect with a content quality effect (the $R \\to Q$ path in Figure~\\ref{fig:dag}).\n17\tTo block this path, we apply a natural language inference (NLI) gate using DeBERTa-v3-large with an entailment threshold of 0.90.\n18\tFor axes that preserve information density (formality, register), we apply standard bidirectional entailment: both NLI(original $\\to$ rewrite) and NLI(rewrite $\\to$ original) must exceed this value.\n19\tFor axes that inherently change information density (e.g., verbosity), bidirectional verification is inappropriate---expansion necessarily introduces new content that the original cannot entail.\n20\tWe instead apply direction-aware verification: for expansions, only backward entailment (rewrite entails original); for compressions, only forward entailment (original entails rewrite).\n21\tThis recovered verbosity from 65\\% to 86\\% pass rate without inflating the effect estimate; for formality, strict bidirectional gating is necessary (Appendix~\\ref{app:gate_validation}).\n22\tThis gating mechanism provides filtering-based confounder control \\citep{veitch2020adapting} by ensuring that retained pairs differ only in style, not in content quality.\n23\tWe additionally compute BERTScore as a supplementary monitoring metric, but it does not participate in the gating decision.\n24\t\n25\t\\subsection{Measurement Protocol}\n26\t\\label{sec:measurement}\n27\t\n28\tEach pair that passes the NLI gate is evaluated by GPT-4o (\\texttt{gpt-4o-2024-08-06}) under two complementary scoring modes.\n29\tIn Likert mode, the judge assigns an integer score from 1 to 10 for overall quality to each text independently.\n30\tIn pairwise mode, the judge receives both texts as candidates A and B and makes a forced choice of which response is better.\n31\t\n32\tTo control for position bias, each pairwise comparison is evaluated twice with the presentation order swapped, which produces $n_{\\text{pairs}} \\times 2$ observations.\n33\tWe apply two analysis strategies to these data.\n34\tFor the nonparametric sign test, we retain only \\emph{consistent} pairs where the judge selected the same text in both orderings.\n35\tFor the Bradley-Terry model, we use all observations including inconsistent pairs, as the model naturally accommodates noise in individual comparisons.\n36\t\n37\t\\subsection{Experimental Setup}\n38\t\\label{sec:setup}\n39\t\n40\tWe evaluate on LLMBar \\citep{zeng2023llmbar}, a benchmark of $n = 100$ instruction-response pairs spanning diverse task types, designed to test LLM judges' ability to distinguish response quality with human-verified quality labels.\n41\tLLMBar is well-suited for style bias detection because its human-verified labels provide ground truth against which judge deviations can be measured, and its moderate size permits exhaustive pairwise evaluation with position control.\n42\t\n43\t\\subsection{Design Rationale}\n44\t\\label{sec:causal_framework}\n45\t\n46\tFigure~\\ref{fig:dag} presents the assumed data-generating process.\n47\tThree variables govern the observed judge score $Y$: the style attribute $S$ (e.g., formality), the original content quality $Q$, and the judge's internal scoring function.\n48\tBoth $S$ and $Q$ causally influence $Y$; the goal of CSD is to approximate the effect $S \\to Y$ while blocking confounding through $Q$.\n49\tStyle-controlled rewriting generates a text pair that differs along a single style axis, but any rewriting operation risks inadvertently altering content quality ($R \\to Q$).\n50\tThe NLI entailment gate provides filtering-based confounder control by removing pairs where the rewrite changed semantic content, thereby blocking the $R \\to Q$ path and yielding a controlled estimate of the $S \\to Y$ effect.\n51\tOur approach provides interventional rather than formal causal evidence: we do not claim that this DAG is a complete structural causal model, nor do we invoke SUTVA or consistency assumptions from the potential outcomes framework.\n52\tOur identification rests on three operational assumptions: (i)~the NLI gate approximates content preservation, blocking the $R \\to Q$ path; (ii)~position swapping controls for presentation-order effects; and (iii)~the style rewrite introduces no systematic differences beyond the target axis.\n53\t\n54\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n55\t\\centering\n56\t\\begin{tikzpicture}[\n57\t    node distance=2.5cm and 3cm,\n58\t    every node/.style={font=\\small},\n59\t    obs/.style={draw, circle, minimum size=1.1cm, thick},\n60\t    mech/.style={draw, rectangle, rounded corners, minimum size=0.9cm, thick, fill=gray!10},\n61\t    gate/.style={draw, rectangle, dashed, thick, fill=blue!8, font=\\footnotesize},\n62\t    arr/.style={-{Stealth[length=5pt]}, thick},\n63\t    blocked/.style={-{Stealth[length=5pt]}, thick, dotted, red!60},\n64\t  ]\n65\t  \\node[obs] (S) {$S$};\n66\t  \\node[obs, right=of S] (Y) {$Y$};\n67\t  \\node[obs, above=1.8cm of Y] (Q) {$Q$};\n68\t  \\node[mech, left=2cm of Q] (R) {Rewrite};\n69\t  \\node[gate, below=0.5cm of R] (NLI) {NLI gate};\n70\t  \\node[obs, below=1.2cm of Y] (P) {$P$};\n71\t\n72\t  \\draw[arr] (S) -- node[below, font=\\footnotesize] {style bias} (Y);\n73\t  \\draw[arr] (Q) -- node[right, font=\\footnotesize] {quality} (Y);\n74\t  \\draw[arr] (R) -- node[above, font=\\footnotesize] {} (S);\n75\t  \\draw[blocked] (R) -- node[above, font=\\footnotesize, black] {\\texttimes} (Q);\n76\t  \\draw[blocked] (R) to[bend left=25] node[pos=0.6, right, font=\\scriptsize, black] {\\texttimes\\ artifact} (Y);\n77\t  \\draw[blocked] (P) -- node[right, font=\\scriptsize, black] {pos.\\ swap} (Y);\n78\t  \\draw[arr, dashed, orange!70!black] (S) to[bend left=30] node[pos=0.5, above left, font=\\scriptsize] {residual confound.} (Q);\n79\t\n80\t  \\draw[arr, gray] (NLI) -- (R);\n81\t\n82\t\\end{tikzpicture}\n83\t\\caption{Assumed data-generating process (design rationale, not a claim of perfect identification). The rewriting mechanism $R$ targets style $S$ but may inadvertently alter content quality $Q$ (dotted red, blocked by NLI gate) or introduce rewriting artifacts ($R \\to Y$, blocked by double-rewrite control). Position bias $P \\to Y$ is controlled by order swapping. The dashed orange $S \\to Q$ path represents residual confounding: style may influence perceived quality through channels the NLI gate cannot block (Limitations). $Y$: judge score.}\n84\t\\label{fig:dag}\n85\t\\end{figure}\n86\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_015zPKBUJH2eqBWZ6KDNPejT","session_id":"05b18906-f618-42bb-ba5a-27a6b0fbf38c","uuid":"7fe65427-0c28-4ed7-8eb7-b36d474a451c","timestamp":"2026-05-21T14:25:01.496Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01DDxpyvZtYfkN9p9M4KXxMw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CNCYe2Um6VLLwxQh596SXW","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_5/projects/causal_style_decomp_judge/docs/paper/sections/related_work.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3011,"cache_read_input_tokens":19660,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3011,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":71,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_015zPKBUJH2eqBWZ6KDNPejT","session_id":"05b18906-f618-42bb-ba5a-27a6b0fbf38c","uuid":"163ace5c-fab8-46f2-a61a-bb2f90acae60"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CNCYe2Um6VLLwxQh596SXW","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Related Work}\n2\t\\label{sec:related_work}\n3\t\n4\tTwo lines of work motivate our approach: interventional methods for diagnosing judge bias, and measurement theory on the sensitivity of different elicitation formats.\n5\t\n6\t\\paragraph{Interventional Approaches to Judge Bias}\n7\tText-based interventional methods have a growing methodological foundation \\citep{feder2021causalm,wu2021polyjuice,feder2022causal}.\n8\t\\citet{reber2025rate} propose RATE, a framework that rewrites model outputs along style attributes using imperfect counterfactuals and measures the causal effect on reward model scores via Likert-scale Average Treatment Effect (ATE).\n9\tRATE applies a correction term to account for rewriting imperfections, but its reliance on Likert ATE as the primary metric is a vulnerability: our experiments show that Likert scoring exhibits a ceiling effect in which 60\\% of style-transformed pairs receive identical integer scores, yielding non-significant ATE estimates ($p = 0.332$) for the same interventions that pairwise comparison detects at $p = 0.006$.\n10\tIf this Likert Blindspot generalizes beyond our setting, RATE's own style-related findings may underestimate the true magnitude of attribute effects.\n11\tCSD differs from RATE in three respects: it targets individual style axes rather than general attributes, it measures bias through position-controlled pairwise comparison rather than Likert ATE, and it enforces content preservation via NLI entailment gating rather than post-hoc correction for imperfect rewrites.\n12\t\\citet{counterfactual2025rhetorical} also adopt an interventional LLM-based design, rewriting texts to measure rhetorical style effects and aggregating preferences with a Bradley-Terry model.\n13\tTheir framework operates at persona-level granularity (e.g., ``writes like a professor''), whereas CSD intervenes on specific style axes (e.g., formality, conciseness) and uses a sign test with position control rather than parametric aggregation.\n14\t\n15\t\\paragraph{Measurement Sensitivity in NLG Evaluation}\n16\tThe distinction between ordinal and interval measurement dates to \\citet{stevens1946theory}, whose taxonomy of scales remains foundational.\n17\tThe superiority of pairwise comparison over rating scales for eliciting reliable human judgments has been established since \\citet{thurstone1927law}, whose Law of Comparative Judgment formalizes why forced-choice comparisons yield finer discriminability than absolute ratings.\n18\t\\citet{preston2000scale} show that scale granularity affects reliability, while \\citet{krosnick1999survey} documents satisficing behavior that may compound the tie inflation we observe.\n19\t\\citet{decoster2011median} show that artificial categorization of continuous variables discards variance and reduces statistical power, directly relevant to the Likert Blindspot mechanism.\n20\tWithin NLP, \\citet{amidei2019likert} review the use of rating and Likert scales in NLG evaluation, identifying inconsistencies in scale design and reporting that compromise cross-study comparability.\n21\t\\citet{clark2021human} demonstrate systematic flaws in human evaluation; \\citet{celikyilmaz2020evaluation} survey evaluation methods, and \\citet{vanderlee2019best,vanderlee2021human} establish best practices for human NLG evaluation.\n22\t\\citet{kiritchenko2017bws} and \\citet{novikova2018rankme} confirm that forced-choice methods (best-worst scaling, ranking) detect quality differences that rating scales miss; \\citet{louviere2015bws} provide the theoretical foundation.\n23\tOur contribution is the first systematic quantification of this gap for LLM judge style bias, identifying the two-layer mechanism (tie inflation plus residual signal attenuation) that explains it.\n24\t\n25\t\\paragraph{Style Bias Detection and Mitigation}\n26\tThe foundational LLM-as-judge framework was established by \\citet{zheng2023judging}, who introduced MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena and documented position bias in pairwise evaluation.\n27\t\\citet{wang2023faireval} further showed that preference rankings can be manipulated by swapping option order, proposing a win/tie/lose annotation scheme.\n28\t\\citet{li2024calibraeval} address selection bias through calibration of prediction distributions during inference.\n29\tThese works focus on \\emph{position} bias and calibration; \\citet{ye2024justice} systematically quantify 12 bias types using automated counterfactual modifications, and \\citet{koo2024cobbler} benchmark cognitive biases in LLM evaluators, though neither provides per-axis isolation of individual style effects.\n30\tStyle bias as a separate, dominant bias source has received attention only recently \\citep{wu2023style,murugadoss2025sos,li2026preference,soumik2026judging}.\n31\t\\citet{li2026preference} further show that style-mediated \\emph{preference leakage} propagates through the evaluation pipeline, motivating per-axis isolation of style effects.\n32\t\\citet{tan2025judgebench} provide JudgeBench, a benchmark revealing that even strong judges perform near chance on challenging pairs, underscoring the need for controlled experimental designs rather than aggregate accuracy metrics.\n33\tMulti-feature frameworks such as CASSE \\citep{casse2026concise} and FairJudge \\citep{wang2026fairjudge} decompose style into finer dimensions but rely on correlational analyses without interventional controls.\n34\t\\citet{stureborg2024inconsistent} demonstrate inconsistency and bias across evaluation formats, and \\citet{park2024offsetbias} show that verbosity and other surface features systematically inflate judge scores.\n35\tCrucially, \\citet{jeong2025comparative} identify the Comparative Trap: pairwise comparison amplifies style biases relative to pointwise scoring, raising the concern that our pairwise-based detection may partly reflect format-level amplification rather than pure detection.\n36\tWe address this directly in \\S\\ref{sec:amplification}.\n37\t\n38\t\\paragraph{Bradley-Terry Models in Evaluation}\n39\tThe Bradley-Terry model \\citep{bradley1952rank} underpins Chatbot Arena \\citep{zheng2023judging,chiang2024chatbot} and AlpacaFarm \\citep{dubois2023alpacafarm}; \\citet{boubdir2023elo} establish best practices, and \\citet{liusie2024comparative} show zero-shot LLM pairwise comparison matches pointwise evaluation.\n40\tWe adopt BT because it uses all comparison data including inconsistent pairs, yielding proper effect-size CIs; our contribution is to measure the Likert--pairwise sensitivity gap for style bias and show that per-axis GEE decomposition reveals confound structures invisible to aggregate scores.\n41\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_015zPKBUJH2eqBWZ6KDNPejT","session_id":"05b18906-f618-42bb-ba5a-27a6b0fbf38c","uuid":"44ea481c-82d0-440f-b3c9-2ae524c47eaa","timestamp":"2026-05-21T14:25:02.299Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01DDxpyvZtYfkN9p9M4KXxMw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01S1ngskp5eFX3C6H8U6qxZX","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_5/projects/causal_style_decomp_judge/docs/paper/sections/experiments.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3011,"cache_read_input_tokens":19660,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3011,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":71,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_015zPKBUJH2eqBWZ6KDNPejT","session_id":"05b18906-f618-42bb-ba5a-27a6b0fbf38c","uuid":"525cffaf-6ba8-4041-902f-25a9f4081606"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01S1ngskp5eFX3C6H8U6qxZX","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Experiments}\n2\t\\label{sec:experiments}\n3\t\n4\t\n5\t\\subsection{Statistical Framework}\n6\t\\label{sec:stat_framework}\n7\t\n8\tWe designate a primary family of six hypothesis tests (two judges $\\times$ three style axes) and control the family-wise error rate with Holm-Bonferroni correction at $\\alpha = 0.05$.\n9\tTable~\\ref{tab:main_results} reports both raw and adjusted $p$-values for this $2 \\times 3$ grid.\n10\tAnalyses outside this grid are classified as either \\emph{exploratory} (NLI threshold sensitivity sweep, \\S\\ref{sec:nli_threshold}) or \\emph{confirmatory} (consistency filter validation, \\S\\ref{sec:consistency}).\n11\tExploratory analyses are reported with uncorrected $p$-values and interpreted as hypothesis-generating; confirmatory analyses test specific hypotheses about pipeline validity and are evaluated at the nominal $\\alpha = 0.05$ level without additional correction, as they address orthogonal questions about measurement reliability rather than the primary style-bias family.\n12\tBradley-Terry parameters are estimated via spectral initialization followed by minorization-maximization \\citep{hunter2004mm}, using the default regularization of the \\texttt{choix} Python library.\n13\tAll bootstrap confidence intervals use 10{,}000 pair-level resamples with seed 42.\n14\t\n15\t\\begin{table}[t]\n16\t\\centering\n17\t\\small\n18\t\\caption{Main results across three judges, three style axes, and three benchmarks. BT: Bradley-Terry win probability (10{,}000 pair-level bootstrap resamples; 95\\% CIs in Appendix~\\ref{app:bt_ci}). AEval: AlpacaEval; MTB: MT-Bench. Claude Sonnet 4 serves as negative control.}\n19\t\\label{tab:main_results}\n20\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lllccc@{}}\n21\t\\toprule\n22\tBench & Judge & Axis & BT $P$ & Likert $d$ ($p$) & \\\\\n23\t\\midrule\n24\tLLMBar & GPT-4o & Form.\\ & .630$^{***}$ & .102 (.332) & $\\dagger$ \\\\\n25\t & GPT-4o & Verb.\\ & .760$^{***}$ & $-$.095 (.403) & $\\dagger$ \\\\\n26\t & GPT-4o & Reg.\\ & .570$^{\\dagger}$ & $-$.031 (.785) & $\\dagger_{\\text{e}}$ \\\\\n27\t\\cmidrule{2-6}\n28\t & Qwen3 & Form.\\ & .665$^{***}$ & .011 (.908) & $\\dagger$ \\\\\n29\t & Qwen3 & Form.$^b$ & .675$^{***}$ & $-$.025 (.600) & $\\dagger$ \\\\\n30\t & Qwen3 & Verb.\\ & .773$^{***}$ & $-$.151 (.179) & $\\dagger$ \\\\\n31\t & Qwen3 & Reg.\\ & .553 & $-$.073 (.505) & $\\dagger_{\\text{e}}$ \\\\\n32\t\\cmidrule{2-6}\n33\t & Claude & Form.\\ & .562 & $-$.021 (.721) & --- \\\\\n34\t & Claude & Verb.\\ & .541 & $-$.039 (.743) & --- \\\\\n35\t\\midrule\n36\tAEval & GPT-4o & Form.\\ & .727$^{***}$ & .148 (.170) & $\\dagger$ \\\\\n37\t & Qwen3 & Form.\\ & .657$^{***}$ & .081 (.198) & $\\dagger$ \\\\\n38\t & Qwen3 & Verb.\\ & .841$^{***}$ & .375 (.0003) & $\\ddagger$ \\\\\n39\t\\midrule\n40\tMTB & GPT-4o & Form.\\ & .692$^{***}$ & .167 (.016) & $\\ddagger$ \\\\\n41\t & Qwen3 & Form.\\ & .712$^{***}$ & .149 (.007) & $\\ddagger$ \\\\\n42\t\\bottomrule\n43\t\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\footnotesize $^{***}$Holm-adj.\\ $p < 0.001$;\\; $^{\\dagger}$raw $p < 0.05$, Holm-adj.\\ n.s.}\\\\\n44\t\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\footnotesize $\\dagger$: Blindspot;\\; $\\ddagger$: both sig.;\\; ---: neither;\\; $\\dagger_{\\text{e}}$: exploratory.}\\\\\n45\t\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\footnotesize $^b$ Scaled replication ($n = 355$); other LLMBar: $n = 88$.}\\\\\n46\t\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\footnotesize Negative $d$: judge rates style-manipulated variants lower on Likert.}\\\\\n47\t\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\footnotesize Empty cells: benchmark--axis combinations not tested; cross-benchmark}\\\\\n48\t\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\footnotesize validation focuses on strongest-evidence configurations.}\n49\t\\end{tabular}\n50\t\\end{table}\n51\t\n52\t\n53\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n54\t\\centering\n55\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/forest_plot.pdf}\n56\t\\caption{Bradley-Terry win probabilities across two judges and three style axes. Filled diamonds: significant ($p < 0.05$); open diamonds: non-significant. Error bars: 95\\% bootstrap CI. The dashed line at 0.50 indicates no style preference. Verbosity shows the strongest and most consistent bias across both judges.}\n57\t\\label{fig:forest_plot}\n58\t\\end{figure}\n59\t\n60\t\\section{Analysis}\n61\t\\label{sec:analysis}\n62\t\n63\t\n64\t\\subsection{NLI Gate Validation}\n65\t\\label{sec:nli_validation}\n66\t\n67\t\\paragraph{False negative rate.}\n68\tWe validate all 100 NLI decisions against an LLM annotator (Qwen3-32B) prompted to judge whether semantic content is preserved.\n69\tThe two systems agree on 91 of 100 pairs (Gwet's AC1 $= 0.89$; Cohen's $\\kappa = 0.43$ is depressed by prevalence skew \\citep{feinstein1990kappa}).\n70\tOf the 12 rejected pairs, 8 are judged content-preserving by the LLM annotator, yielding an overall false negative rate of $8/95 = 8.4\\%$.\n71\tThree robustness checks confirm these do not compromise conclusions: win rate stable across NLI thresholds (72.3--73.9\\%, all $p < 0.005$; \\S\\ref{sec:nli_threshold}), no covariate differences between retained and discarded pairs (all $p > 0.12$; \\S\\ref{sec:consistency}), and no directional bias in false negatives (Fisher $p = 0.35$).\n72\tThe NLI gate operates as a precision-oriented conservative filter (precision $= 98.9\\%$); verbosity-rejected pairs have larger edit distances (Cliff's $\\delta = +0.45$, $p < 0.001$), making estimates lower bounds.\n73\tFull-scale GPT-4o validation on all 200 NLI gate decisions yields 92.1\\% pass precision for formality and 82.6\\% for verbosity, with the lower verbosity figure reflecting the permissiveness of direction-aware gating (17.4\\% false positives vs.\\ 7.9\\% for the strict bidirectional formality gate---a trade-off for the higher pass rate that enables adequate sample sizes).\n74\t\n75\t\\paragraph{Threshold sensitivity.}\n76\t\\label{sec:nli_threshold}\n77\tThe formality win rate is stable at 72.3--73.9\\% across NLI thresholds of 0.70, 0.90, and 0.95 (sign test $p < 0.005$ at every threshold).\n78\tNLI scores follow a bimodal distribution (most pairs above 0.95 or below 0.30), so the 0.90 threshold lies on a plateau rather than a cliff edge.\n79\t\n80\t\n81\t\\subsection{Measurement Sensitivity: Likert vs.\\ Pairwise}\n82\t\\label{sec:likert_pairwise}\n83\t\n84\t\\paragraph{Head-to-head comparison with RATE.}\n85\tApplying RATE's Likert ATE methodology \\citep{reber2025rate} to our formality style-transformed pairs yields Cohen's $d = 0.10$, $p = 0.33$ (non-significant, $n = 88$); pairwise comparison on identical data reveals a 63.0\\% formal preference ($p = 0.0004$, Bradley-Terry on all 173 comparisons), confirmed by a consistency-filtered sign test (73.9\\%, $p = 0.006$, $n = 46$ consistent pairs).\n86\tThis is the Likert Blindspot: the same data, the same style intervention, two measurement instruments---one detects bias, the other does not.\n87\tA $4\\times$ scaled replication ($n = 355$) distinguishes measurement attenuation from insufficient power: the Likert effect size shrinks to $d = {-}0.025$ (near zero) while BT remains stable at $67.5\\%$ ($p < 0.0001$)---if the gap were merely statistical power, $d$ would remain non-zero at larger $n$.\n88\tAttenuation follows a severity spectrum.\n89\tAt moderate bias (BT 63--67\\%), it is complete: $d$ converges to ${\\approx}0$ even at $n = 355$, rendering the bias invisible to any Likert-based test.\n90\tAt stronger effects (BT ${>}70\\%$), Likert partially detects but severely attenuates: MT-Bench formality yields Likert $d \\approx 0.15$ for BT ${\\sim}70\\%$ effects---${>}60\\%$ signal compression---explaining why three of nine bias-positive cells escape the Blindspot (Table~\\ref{tab:main_results}, $\\ddagger$ rows).\n91\t\n92\tThe two frameworks are complementary: RATE measures aggregate quality effects via Likert ATE; CSD targets per-axis style preference via forced-choice comparison, which eliminates ties and is more sensitive to ordinal preferences.\n93\tThe Blindspot arises because Likert tie rates cluster at 58--73\\% across all six judge--axis configurations---a structural property of integer scales that collapses real preference signal into uninformative ties before any statistical test is applied.\n94\t\n95\t\n96\t\\paragraph{Sensitivity hierarchy across measurement methods.}\n97\tTo confirm that the Likert Blindspot is not an artifact of a particular statistical test, we apply seven measurement methods to the same formality data and rank them by sensitivity (Table~\\ref{tab:sensitivity}).\n98\tBoth pairwise methods detect the formality preference; all Likert-based methods fail regardless of whether they assume interval scaling (ATE $t$-test), ordinal scaling (ordinal logistic regression), or nonparametric ranking (Wilcoxon signed-rank).\n99\tThe same attenuation pattern replicates on verbosity (Appendix~\\ref{app:verb_sensitivity}), where all six Likert methods remain non-significant despite a stronger pairwise signal (BT 77.3\\%, $p < 0.001$).\n100\t\n101\t\\begin{table}[t]\n102\t\\centering\n103\t\\small\n104\t\\caption{Sensitivity hierarchy: seven measurement methods applied to the same formality style-transformed data. Only pairwise methods (top two rows) reach significance. All Likert-based methods fail.}\n105\t\\label{tab:sensitivity}\n106\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n107\t\\toprule\n108\tMethod & Statistic & $p$ & Data \\\\\n109\t\\midrule\n110\tBT model & 63.0\\% $[55.7, 70.3]$ & $0.0004$ & All 173 comp. \\\\\n111\tSign test (filtered) & 73.9\\% (34/46) & 0.006 & Consistent \\\\\n112\t\\midrule\n113\tLikert ATE ($t$-test) & $d = 0.102$ & 0.332 & NLI$\\geq$0.90 \\\\\n114\tBinarized Likert & 60.6\\% & 0.296 & Non-tied \\\\\n115\tOrdinal reg.\\ (prop.\\ odds) & $\\beta = {-}0.079$ & 0.769 & NLI$\\geq$0.90 \\\\\n116\tGEE (exchangeable) & $\\beta = {-}0.084$ & 0.587 & NLI$\\geq$0.90 \\\\\n117\tWilcoxon signed-rank & $W = 274$ & 0.905 & NLI$\\geq$0.90 \\\\\n118\t\\bottomrule\n119\t\\end{tabular}\n120\t\\end{table}\n121\t\n122\tFigure~\\ref{fig:power_curve} plots detection probability curves at observed effect magnitudes (not post-hoc power; cf.\\ \\citealt{hoenig2001abuse}): Likert requires ${\\approx}4.5\\times$ more data to detect the same bias.\n123\tAn equally plausible interpretation is that Likert and pairwise modes measure fundamentally different constructs (absolute quality vs.\\ relative preference) rather than reflecting measurement failure; both interpretations imply that Likert-only evaluation misses style-driven preference patterns that matter for deployment.\n124\t\n125\t\\paragraph{Detection vs.\\ amplification.}\n126\t\\label{sec:amplification}\n127\t\\citet{jeong2025comparative} show that pairwise comparison amplifies style biases (the Comparative Trap).\n128\tFour observations bound this risk: (i)~NLI gate ablation shows formality NLI-fail pairs \\emph{reverse} to BT 41.7\\% while NLI-pass pairs show 66.5\\%---the gate filters content noise, not amplifies style signal (Appendix~\\ref{app:nli_ablation}); (ii)~Claude Sonnet 4 shows no bias with the identical pairwise format (BT 56.2\\%/54.1\\%, n.s.); (iii)~the cross-axis gradient (register 55\\% $<$ formality 67\\% $<$ verbosity 77\\%) correlates with manipulation invasiveness rather than uniform amplification; (iv)~debiased prompting reduces Qwen3-32B's BT from 66.5\\% to 60.8\\%, inconsistent with format-fabricated bias.\n129\tCSD cannot fully separate detection from amplification of micro-preferences, but the practical implication is identical.\n130\t\n131\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n132\t\\centering\n133\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/power_curve.pdf}\n134\t\\caption{Detection probability at observed effect magnitudes. 80\\% power crossings: sign test $n \\approx 62$, BT $n \\approx 114$, binarized Likert $n \\approx 462$, Likert ATE $n \\approx 1{,}473$. At typical scales ($n = 50$--$200$, shaded), pairwise methods reach 80\\% power while Likert methods remain far below. At $n = 355$, Likert $d = {-}0.025$ confirms measurement attenuation rather than a mere power gap.}\n135\t\\label{fig:power_curve}\n136\t\\end{figure}\n137\t\n138\t\\subsection{Cross-Judge Replication: Qwen3-32B}\n139\t\\label{sec:cross_judge}\n140\t\n141\tQwen3-32B replicates the Blindspot: BT 66.5\\% ($[58.5\\%, 73.9\\%]$, $p < 0.0001$) while Likert $d = 0.011$ ($p = 0.908$).\n142\tThe higher estimate may partly reflect evaluator-producer bias (Qwen3-32B serves as both rewriter and judge; cross-rewriter validation in Appendix~\\ref{app:rewriter_indep}).\n143\tVerbosity ORs are highly consistent across judges ($11.34$ vs.\\ $13.10$; Table~\\ref{tab:gee}).\n144\t\n145\t\\subsection{Cross-Axis Replication: Verbosity}\n146\t\\label{sec:cross_axis}\n147\t\n148\tThe verbosity axis provides the strongest and cleanest demonstration of the Blindspot.\n149\tBoth judges show near-identical verbosity preference: GPT-4o 76.0\\% (BT, bootstrap 95\\% CI: $[66.7\\%, 85.3\\%]$, $p = 0.0001$) and Qwen3-32B 77.3\\% ($[70.3\\%, 84.3\\%]$, $p < 0.0001$), while Likert ATE remains non-significant for both ($d = -0.095$, $p = 0.403$; $d = -0.151$, $p = 0.179$).\n150\tUnlike formality, where the style effect is confounded with originality, verbosity exhibits no observed originality confound for either judge: GEE decomposition confirms massive style effects (Qwen3-32B $\\text{OR} = 13.10$; GPT-4o $\\text{OR} = 11.34$; both $p < .0001$) while the originality component is non-significant in both (Qwen3-32B $\\text{OR} = 0.49$, $p = .096$; GPT-4o $\\text{OR} = 0.45$, $p = .062$; Table~\\ref{tab:gee}).\n151\tBoth rewriting directions favor verbose responses, with the concise$\\to$verbose direction (90.7\\%) even exceeding verbose$\\to$concise (80.6\\%), providing convergent directional and GEE evidence that the full 77.3\\% effect is attributable to style rather than originality.\n152\tControlling for length (ratio, difference, log-ratio) leaves the style OR virtually unchanged ($<$2\\%; all $p > 0.27$).\n153\t\n154\t\\subsection{Register: Weak Evidence with Strong Originality Confound}\n155\t\\label{sec:negative_control}\n156\t\n157\tRegister provides weak but directionally consistent evidence (GPT-4o BT 57.0\\%, $p = 0.04$; Qwen3-32B 55.3\\%, $p = 0.076$; Likert n.s.).\n158\tGEE decomposition reveals the strongest originality confound ($\\text{OR} = 5.72$, $p < 0.0001$) alongside a marginally significant style effect ($\\text{OR} = 1.91$, $p = 0.046$; Table~\\ref{tab:gee}).\n159\tThis exploratory finding does not enter the primary Holm-Bonferroni family.\n160\t\n161\t\\subsection{GEE Conditional Decomposition and Confound Structure}\n162\t\\label{sec:direction}\n163\t\n164\tDirection analysis reveals partial confounding between style and original quality across axes; GEE conditional decomposition \\citep{liang1986gee} separates the two effects (full direction-asymmetry analysis in Appendix~\\ref{app:direction}).\n165\tAfter controlling for original preference, the formality effect remains significant for both judges (GPT-4o: $\\text{OR} = 2.58$, $p = 0.002$; Qwen3-32B: $\\text{OR} = 2.15$, $p < 0.0001$, $n = 355$; Table~\\ref{tab:gee}); the significant originality OR ($2.32$, $p = 0.006$) confirms residual $R \\to Q$ confounding, so this is a partial rather than clean separation.\n166\tA $4\\times$ scaled replication ($n = 355$) confirms the Blindspot: BT stable at $67.5\\%$ (halved CI), Likert still non-significant ($p = 0.60$); the OR decrease ($4.20 \\to 2.15$) reflects small-sample correction.\n167\t\n168\t\\begin{table}[t]\n169\t\\centering\n170\t\\small\n171\t\\caption{GEE conditional decomposition (exchangeable correlation) of style and originality effects. Verbosity shows no observed originality confound; formality and register show comparable style effects with varying originality confounds.}\n172\t\\label{tab:gee}\n173\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}llclc@{}}\n174\t\\toprule\n175\t & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{Style OR} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{Originality OR} \\\\\n176\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-3} \\cmidrule(lr){4-5}\n177\tConfig & OR & $p$ & OR & $p$ \\\\\n178\t\\midrule\n179\tQwen3 verb.\\ & 13.10 & ${<}.0001$ & 0.49 & .096 \\\\\n180\tGPT-4o verb.\\ & 11.34 & ${<}.0001$ & 0.45 & .062 \\\\\n181\t\\midrule\n182\tGPT-4o form.\\ & 2.58 & .002 & 2.32 & .006 \\\\\n183\tQwen3 form.\\ & 4.20 & .0002 & 4.70 & .0001 \\\\\n184\tQwen3 form.$^a$ & 2.15 & ${<}.0001$ & 4.99 & ${<}.0001$ \\\\\n185\t\\midrule\n186\tGPT-4o reg.\\ & 1.88 & .029 & 2.21 & .006 \\\\\n187\tQwen3 reg.\\ & 1.91 & .046 & 5.72 & ${<}.0001$ \\\\\n188\t\\bottomrule\n189\t\\multicolumn{5}{l}{\\footnotesize $^a$ $n = 355$; other rows: $n = 88$. 95\\% CIs in Appendix.}\n190\t\\end{tabular}\n191\t\\end{table}\n192\t\n193\tThe confound structure reveals two tiers: verbosity shows no observed originality confound (Style OR $= 13.10$ / $11.34$, Originality n.s.); formality and register show comparable style effects (OR $\\approx 1.9$--$2.6$) alongside significant originality confounds.\n194\tCross-benchmark replication confirms formality bias across all six benchmark$\\times$judge configurations (BT 63--73\\%, all $p < 0.001$; AlpacaEval verbosity: BT 84.1\\%, Style OR $= 28.64$); Claude Sonnet 4 shows no style preference (BT 56.2\\%/54.1\\%, n.s.) but extreme originality sensitivity (Orig OR $= 6.66$, $p < 0.0001$).\n195\tThe Blindspot manifests in six of nine bias-positive cells; three exceptions (BT $> 69\\%$, Likert $d \\geq 0.14$) confirm that only extreme effects cross Likert's threshold.\n196\tSame-style double-rewrite controls confirm a rewriting artifact (Appendix~\\ref{app:artifact_control}), validating GEE decomposition: the artifact cancels in GEE's originality term, leaving Style ORs uncontaminated.\n197\tA debiased prompt reduces Qwen3-32B's BT from $66.5\\%$ to $60.8\\%$ ($p = 0.0023$) with negligible GPT-4o effect; prompt-level debiasing is insufficient.\n198\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_015zPKBUJH2eqBWZ6KDNPejT","session_id":"05b18906-f618-42bb-ba5a-27a6b0fbf38c","uuid":"eaf6a962-9751-49ea-8d04-6fd71f8f9cb2","timestamp":"2026-05-21T14:25:02.382Z"}
