{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XHf7pLqu9439TAXY3BETKR","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Conclusion}\n2\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n3\t\n4\tWe introduced Controlled Style Decomposition (CSD), a black-box framework that reveals the Likert Blindspot---systematic measurement attenuation in Likert scoring---across three judges, three benchmarks, and three style axes.\n5\tThe Blindspot is not merely a statistical power gap: at moderate bias levels (BT 63--67\\%), Likert compresses effect sizes to zero even at $n = 355$; at stronger effects, it attenuates by over 60\\%.\n6\tSeven statistical methods confirm this is structural, not an artifact of any particular test.\n7\t\n8\tVerbosity provides the cleanest evidence: both judges show 76--77\\% pairwise preference for verbose responses with no observed originality confound (Originality n.s.), yet Likert detects nothing.\n9\tGEE conditional decomposition reveals that formality and register exhibit conditional style effects alongside originality confounds, motivating per-axis analysis over aggregate style metrics.\n10\t\n11\tFor the evaluation community, the practical implication is clear: style bias audits should adopt content-controlled pairwise comparison (with NLI gating or equivalent), especially at the moderate effect sizes typical of real-world evaluation ($n < 500$), while acknowledging that format-level amplification of micro-preferences cannot be fully excluded \\citep{jeong2025comparative}.\n12\tExisting Likert-based null findings may reflect measurement insensitivity rather than genuine absence of bias.\n13\tFuture work should incorporate human evaluation of rewriting quality, broader benchmark and axis coverage, and stronger causal identification through randomized rather than observational designs.\n14\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_015zPKBUJH2eqBWZ6KDNPejT","session_id":"05b18906-f618-42bb-ba5a-27a6b0fbf38c","uuid":"eeaa867b-aace-4195-8dac-3e10a4c91b75","timestamp":"2026-05-21T14:25:06.714Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01WjnvSKCsJUxptnjpHzbA25","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VFPJtB7XNd8RXauTUUocqK","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_5/projects/causal_style_decomp_judge/docs/paper/sections/appendix.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10450,"cache_read_input_tokens":22671,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":10450,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":38,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_015zPKBUJH2eqBWZ6KDNPejT","session_id":"05b18906-f618-42bb-ba5a-27a6b0fbf38c","uuid":"3abfff57-ae53-4f3b-a3da-b261d39329a1"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VFPJtB7XNd8RXauTUUocqK","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\appendix\n2\t\n3\t\\section{Direction Asymmetry and Quality Confounding}\n4\t\\label{app:direction}\n5\t\n6\tThe formality axis exhibits a pronounced direction asymmetry: the judge selects the formal variant in 78.8\\% of formal-is-rewrite pairs ($p = 0.001$) but only 54.5\\% of casual-is-rewrite pairs ($p = 0.83$).\n7\tThis asymmetry suggests that original quality $Q$ and formality $S$ are partially confounded---formally written texts may be inherently perceived as higher quality.\n8\tTo quantify this, we fit a GEE logistic model (exchangeable correlation) with both formality and original-preference as predictors.\n9\tAfter controlling for original preference, the formality effect remains significant ($\\text{OR} = 2.58$, $p = 0.002$), and the original-preference effect is also significant ($\\text{OR} = 2.32$, $p = 0.006$).\n10\tThe formality preference thus survives adjustment for original-preference confounding, though the significant original-preference effect indicates that quality--style confounding is present and non-negligible.\n11\t\n12\tAt $n = 88$, the Qwen3-32B formality Style OR ($4.20$) exceeded the GPT-4o estimate ($2.58$), but a scaled replication ($n = 355$) corrects this to $2.15$ ($p < 0.0001$), comparable to GPT-4o and consistent with small-sample upward bias rather than evaluator-producer inflation of the style effect.\n13\tThe originality confound remains stronger ($4.99$ vs.\\ $2.32$), consistent with evaluator-producer bias inflating the originality component (Limitations).\n14\tThe directional agreement between judges---both favor formal text after controlling for originality---supports the existence of formality bias, but the effect magnitude from Qwen3-32B should not be directly compared with GPT-4o.\n15\t\n16\tThe NLI gate blocks \\emph{content} changes but cannot block quality perception shifts that are themselves mediated by style---a fundamental identifiability limit of any text-rewriting approach.\n17\t\n18\t\\section{Consistency Filter Robustness}\n19\t\\label{sec:consistency}\n20\t\n21\tThe position-consistency filter retains 55 of 98 valid pairs (56.1\\%); consistent and inconsistent groups show no significant differences across four covariates (NLI score $p = 0.824$, BERTScore $p = 0.12$, text length $p = 0.137$, length diff $p = 0.715$), indicating selection on judge decisiveness rather than text properties.\n22\tThe BT model using all 173 comparisons including inconsistent pairs yields 63.0\\% ($p = 0.0004$), consistent with the filtered sign test (73.9\\%, $p = 0.006$), confirming no systematic bias from the filter.\n23\t\n24\t\\section{GEE Model Diagnostics}\n25\t\\label{app:gee_diagnostics}\n26\t\n27\t\\paragraph{Correlation structure robustness.}\n28\tTable~\\ref{tab:gee_corr} compares exchangeable and independence working correlation structures across all six judge$\\times$axis cells.\n29\tThe two structures yield virtually identical results: $|\\Delta\\text{QIC}| < 0.001$ and maximum coefficient change ${<}0.02$ in every cell, with estimated within-pair correlations $\\hat{\\alpha} \\in [-0.25, +0.11]$.\n30\tAll GEE results reported in the main text are therefore robust to correlation structure specification.\n31\t\n32\t\\begin{table}[h]\n33\t\\centering\n34\t\\small\n35\t\\caption{GEE correlation structure comparison across six judge$\\times$axis cells.}\n36\t\\label{tab:gee_corr}\n37\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n38\t\\toprule\n39\tConfig & Exch.\\ QIC & Indep.\\ QIC & $|\\Delta\\text{QIC}|$ & Max$|\\Delta\\text{Coef}|$ \\\\\n40\t\\midrule\n41\tGPT-4o form.\\ & --- & --- & ${<}0.001$ & ${<}0.02$ \\\\\n42\tQwen3 form.\\ & --- & --- & ${<}0.001$ & ${<}0.02$ \\\\\n43\tGPT-4o verb.\\ & --- & --- & ${<}0.001$ & ${<}0.02$ \\\\\n44\tQwen3 verb.\\ & --- & --- & ${<}0.001$ & ${<}0.02$ \\\\\n45\tGPT-4o reg.\\ & --- & --- & ${<}0.001$ & ${<}0.02$ \\\\\n46\tQwen3 reg.\\ & --- & --- & ${<}0.001$ & ${<}0.02$ \\\\\n47\t\\bottomrule\n48\t\\end{tabular}\n49\t\\end{table}\n50\t\n51\t\\paragraph{Interaction test.}\n52\tTo verify that the additive GEE specification is appropriate, we test a style$\\times$originality interaction term on formality data for both judges.\n53\tThe interaction is non-significant in both cases (GPT-4o: $p = 0.53$; Qwen3-32B: $p = 0.32$), confirming that the additive model is the correct specification and that style and originality effects operate independently.\n54\t\n55\t\\paragraph{95\\% confidence intervals.}\n56\tTable~\\ref{tab:gee_ci} reports 95\\% CIs for all GEE odds ratios in Table~\\ref{tab:gee}.\n57\t\n58\t\\begin{table}[h]\n59\t\\centering\n60\t\\small\n61\t\\caption{95\\% CIs for GEE conditional decomposition (Table~\\ref{tab:gee}).}\n62\t\\label{tab:gee_ci}\n63\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcc@{}}\n64\t\\toprule\n65\tConfig & Style OR [CI] & Orig OR [CI] \\\\\n66\t\\midrule\n67\tQwen3 verb.\\ & 13.10 [5.62, 30.55] & 0.49 [0.21, 1.13] \\\\\n68\tGPT-4o verb.\\ & 11.34 & 0.45 \\\\\n69\tGPT-4o form.\\ & 2.58 & 2.32 \\\\\n70\tQwen3 form.\\ & 4.20 [1.99, 8.87] & 4.70 \\\\\n71\tQwen3 form.$^a$ & 2.15 [1.46, 3.16] & 4.99 [3.40, 7.34] \\\\\n72\tGPT-4o reg.\\ & 1.88 & 2.21 \\\\\n73\tQwen3 reg.\\ & 1.91 [1.01, 3.58] & 5.72 [3.02, 10.82] \\\\\n74\t\\bottomrule\n75\t\\multicolumn{3}{l}{\\footnotesize $^a$ $n = 355$; other rows: $n = 88$.}\n76\t\\end{tabular}\n77\t\\end{table}\n78\t\n79\t\\section{Rewriter Independence}\n80\t\\label{app:rewriter_indep}\n81\t\n82\tTo test whether the Blindspot depends on the rewriter's identity, we replace Qwen3-32B with GPT-4o as rewriter while keeping Qwen3-32B as judge.\n83\t\n84\t\\paragraph{Verbosity (Table~\\ref{tab:rewriter_indep}).}\n85\tThe Style OR decreases by $22\\%$ ($13.10 \\to 10.17$), bounding evaluator-producer inflation of the style effect; the pure-style pattern (Originality n.s.) and Blindspot (BT significant, Likert n.s.) both persist.\n86\t\n87\t\\begin{table}[h]\n88\t\\centering\n89\t\\small\n90\t\\caption{Rewriter independence test: verbosity axis, Qwen3-32B judge. GPT-4o rewriter eliminates evaluator-producer overlap while preserving the Blindspot.}\n91\t\\label{tab:rewriter_indep}\n92\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n93\t\\toprule\n94\t & GPT-4o Rewriter & Qwen3 Rewriter \\\\\n95\t\\midrule\n96\tNLI pass rate & 81/100 (81\\%) & 86/100 (86\\%) \\\\\n97\tBT $P$ [95\\% CI] & .735 [.654, .809] & .773 [.703, .843] \\\\\n98\tLikert ATE ($p$) & .000 (.838) & ${\\sim}$0 (.179) \\\\\n99\tBlindspot & Yes & Yes \\\\\n100\tGEE Style OR ($p$) & 10.17 (${<}.0001$) & 13.10 (${<}.0001$) \\\\\n101\tGEE Orig OR ($p$) & 1.61 (n.s.) & 0.49 (n.s.) \\\\\n102\t\\bottomrule\n103\t\\end{tabular}\n104\t\\end{table}\n105\t\n106\t\\paragraph{Formality (Table~\\ref{tab:rewriter_indep_form}).}\n107\tGPT-4o rewriter produces formality-manipulated pairs yielding BT $64.4\\%$ $[56.1, 72.8]$, statistically indistinguishable from the Qwen3-32B baseline ($66.5\\%$ $[58.5, 73.9]$; $\\Delta = -2.1$pp).\n108\tThe Likert Blindspot replicates with the independent rewriter ($p = 0.184$, n.s.).\n109\t\n110\t\\begin{table}[h]\n111\t\\centering\n112\t\\small\n113\t\\caption{Rewriter independence test: formality axis, Qwen3-32B judge.}\n114\t\\label{tab:rewriter_indep_form}\n115\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n116\t\\toprule\n117\t & GPT-4o Rewriter & Qwen3 Rewriter \\\\\n118\t\\midrule\n119\tNLI pass rate & 90/100 (90\\%) & 88/100 (88\\%) \\\\\n120\tBT $P$ [95\\% CI] & .644 [.561, .728] & .665 [.585, .739] \\\\\n121\tLikert $d$ ($p$) & $-$.081 (.184) & .011 (.908) \\\\\n122\tBlindspot & Yes & Yes \\\\\n123\t\\bottomrule\n124\t\\end{tabular}\n125\t\\end{table}\n126\t\n127\t\\section{Direction-Aware Gate Validation}\n128\t\\label{app:gate_validation}\n129\t\n130\tFor verbosity, the direction-aware NLI gate admits 21 additional pairs (32\\%) compared to strict bidirectional verification.\n131\tThese pairs show consistent preference direction (BT 73.8\\% vs.\\ 78.5\\% for bidirectional-pass pairs), and overall BT estimates are statistically indistinguishable ($\\Delta = {-}1.1$pp, 95\\% CI $[{-}5.6, {+}3.0]$), confirming that direction-aware gating recovers valid pairs without inflating the effect.\n132\tFor formality, strict bidirectional verification is appropriate: a relaxed (direction-aware) gate would admit 9 additional pairs with reversed preference (BT 33.4\\%), indicating content preservation failure rather than genuine style preference.\n133\tThis asymmetry empirically justifies the axis-dependent gating strategy described in Section~\\ref{sec:nli_gate}.\n134\t\n135\t\\section{Claude Sonnet 4 Equivalence Testing}\n136\t\\label{app:claude_tost}\n137\t\n138\tWe failed to detect style bias for Claude at the current sample size ($n = 87$; BT $p = 0.091$, $p = 0.284$).\n139\tFormal equivalence testing \\citep[TOST;][]{lakens2017equivalence} at $\\Delta = 10$pp is underpowered at $n = 87$; at $\\Delta = 15$pp (corresponding to a small-to-medium effect in evaluation contexts) both axes confirm equivalence, bounding any Claude style preference to ${<}15$pp---substantively distinct from GPT-4o ($13$--$23$pp) and Qwen3-32B ($17$--$27$pp).\n140\t\n141\t\\section{Bradley-Terry Goodness of Fit and Stratified Analysis}\n142\t\\label{app:bt_gof}\n143\t\n144\tBradley-Terry goodness-of-fit tests confirm adequate model fit for both axes (formality: deviance $= 52.80$, $\\text{df} = 45$, $p = 0.198$; verbosity: $p = 0.905$).\n145\tStratified analysis of consistent pairs by original-text direction reveals no preference heterogeneity for verbosity (Fisher $p = 0.453$) but significant direction asymmetry for formality ($88.9\\%$ vs.\\ $52.6\\%$, Fisher $p = 0.015$), independently confirming the originality confound structure identified by GEE (Table~\\ref{tab:gee}).\n146\t\n147\tTo assess robustness to originality confounding, we restrict to pairs where the judge assigned equal Likert scores to original and counterfactual texts (eliminating detectable quality differences).\n148\tStyle preference persists: formality BT $= 67.3\\%$ ($n = 55$, $\\Delta = {+}0.8$pp from full sample, 95\\% CI $[{-}11.0, {+}12.6]$); verbosity BT $= 76.0\\%$ ($n = 50$, $\\Delta = {-}1.3$pp, 95\\% CI $[{-}13.3, {+}10.3]$).\n149\tIn the anti-originality subset (pairs where the original never wins), effects strengthen (formality $75.0\\%$, verbosity $86.1\\%$), confirming that style preference is not an artifact of originality confounding.\n150\t\n151\t\\section{Bradley-Terry Confidence Intervals}\n152\t\\label{app:bt_ci}\n153\t\n154\tTable~\\ref{tab:bt_ci} reports bootstrap 95\\% confidence intervals for all Bradley-Terry estimates in Table~\\ref{tab:main_results}.\n155\t\n156\t\\begin{table}[h]\n157\t\\centering\n158\t\\small\n159\t\\caption{Bootstrap 95\\% CIs for BT win probabilities (10{,}000 pair-level resamples).}\n160\t\\label{tab:bt_ci}\n161\t\\begin{tabular}{lllc}\n162\t\\toprule\n163\tBench & Judge & Axis & BT $P$ [95\\% CI] \\\\\n164\t\\midrule\n165\tLLMBar & GPT-4o & Form.\\ & .630 [.557, .703] \\\\\n166\t & GPT-4o & Verb.\\ & .760 [.667, .853] \\\\\n167\t & GPT-4o & Reg.\\ & .570 [.468, .673] \\\\\n168\t\\cmidrule{2-4}\n169\t & Qwen3 & Form.\\ & .665 [.585, .739] \\\\\n170\t & Qwen3 & Verb.\\ & .773 [.703, .843] \\\\\n171\t & Qwen3 & Reg.\\ & .553 [.479, .628] \\\\\n172\t\\cmidrule{2-4}\n173\t & Claude & Form.\\ & .562 [.489, .636] \\\\\n174\t & Claude & Verb.\\ & .541 [.459, .616] \\\\\n175\t\\midrule\n176\tAEval & GPT-4o & Form.\\ & .727 [.663, .785] \\\\\n177\t & Qwen3 & Form.\\ & .657 [.581, .733] \\\\\n178\t & Qwen3 & Verb.\\ & .841 [.774, .902] \\\\\n179\t\\midrule\n180\tMTB & GPT-4o & Form.\\ & .692 [.623, .753] \\\\\n181\t & Qwen3 & Form.\\ & .712 [.630, .788] \\\\\n182\t\\bottomrule\n183\t\\end{tabular}\n184\t\\end{table}\n185\t\n186\t\\section{Verbosity Sensitivity Hierarchy}\n187\t\\label{app:verb_sensitivity}\n188\t\n189\tTable~\\ref{tab:verb_sensitivity} replicates the seven-method sensitivity analysis (Table~\\ref{tab:sensitivity}) on the verbosity axis ($n = 86$ NLI-filtered pairs, Qwen3-32B judge, LLMBar).\n190\tThe attenuation pattern is more extreme than formality: all six Likert-based methods remain non-significant (lowest $p = 0.213$) despite a stronger pairwise signal (BT 77.3\\% vs.\\ 66.5\\% for formality), and Likert effect sizes ($d = 0.036$--$0.056$) are near zero.\n191\t\n192\t\\begin{table}[h]\n193\t\\centering\n194\t\\small\n195\t\\caption{Sensitivity hierarchy for verbosity ($n = 86$, Qwen3-32B, LLMBar). All Likert methods fail; only BT reaches significance.}\n196\t\\label{tab:verb_sensitivity}\n197\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n198\t\\toprule\n199\tMethod & Statistic & $p$ & Sig? \\\\\n200\t\\midrule\n201\tBT model & 77.3\\% $[70.3, 84.3]$ & ${<}0.001$ & Yes \\\\\n202\t\\midrule\n203\tLikert ATE & $d = 0.056$ & 0.691 & No \\\\\n204\tBinarized $\\chi^2$ & OR $= 2.16$ & 0.213 & No \\\\\n205\tOrdinal reg.\\ & $\\beta = 0.159$ & 0.575 & No \\\\\n206\tPaired $t$-test & $d = 0.056$ & 0.607 & No \\\\\n207\tMann-Whitney $U$ & $r = {-}0.046$ & 0.578 & No \\\\\n208\tCohen's $d$ & $d = 0.036$ & --- & No \\\\\n209\t\\bottomrule\n210\t\\multicolumn{4}{p{0.9\\columnwidth}}{\\footnotesize Effect sizes use style-aligned scoring (verbose $-$ concise), isolating style preference direction. Table~\\ref{tab:main_results} reports RATE-compatible ATE (counterfactual $-$ original), which conflates style and originality effects.}\n211\t\\end{tabular}\n212\t\\end{table}\n213\t\n214\t\\section{NLI Gate Ablation}\n215\t\\label{app:nli_ablation}\n216\t\n217\tTable~\\ref{tab:nli_ablation} compares BT estimates across NLI-pass, NLI-fail, and all pairs.\n218\tFor formality, NLI-fail pairs reverse to 41.7\\% (preferring casual over formal), demonstrating that the gate removes content-confounded pairs rather than amplifying style signal.\n219\tThe small NLI-fail sample ($n \\approx 12$) limits precision; this serves as a bounding analysis.\n220\tFor verbosity, NLI-fail pairs remain at 75.0\\%, consistent with the dominant style effect (OR $= 13.10$) overwhelming content noise.\n221\t\n222\t\\begin{table}[h]\n223\t\\centering\n224\t\\small\n225\t\\caption{NLI gate ablation: BT win probability by NLI subset.}\n226\t\\label{tab:nli_ablation}\n227\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n228\t\\toprule\n229\tAxis & NLI-pass & NLI-fail & All \\\\\n230\t\\midrule\n231\tFormality & 66.5\\% & 41.7\\% & 63.0\\% \\\\\n232\tVerbosity & 77.3\\% & 75.0\\% & 76.0\\% \\\\\n233\t\\bottomrule\n234\t\\end{tabular}\n235\t\\end{table}\n236\t\n237\t\\section{Rewriting Artifact Control}\n238\t\\label{app:artifact_control}\n239\t\n240\tSame-style double-rewrite (DR) controls quantify the rewriting artifact: the proportion of pairs where the judge prefers the original over its same-style DR.\n241\tBoth judges systematically prefer LLM-polished text across axes (Table~\\ref{tab:artifact}).\n242\tFor verbosity, the near-zero original win rate indicates that rewriting quality dominates when content is held constant, validating the GEE decomposition that separates style from artifact effects.\n243\t\n244\t\\begin{table}[h]\n245\t\\centering\n246\t\\small\n247\t\\caption{Rewriting artifact: original win rate vs.\\ same-style double-rewrite.}\n248\t\\label{tab:artifact}\n249\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n250\t\\toprule\n251\tAxis & GPT-4o & Qwen3-32B \\\\\n252\t\\midrule\n253\tFormality & 31.8\\% ($p = .023$) & 26.7\\% ($p = .003$) \\\\\n254\tVerbosity & 6.2\\% ($p < .001$) & 0\\% ($p < .001$) \\\\\n255\t\\bottomrule\n256\t\\end{tabular}\n257\t\\end{table}\n258\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_015zPKBUJH2eqBWZ6KDNPejT","session_id":"05b18906-f618-42bb-ba5a-27a6b0fbf38c","uuid":"38f80010-897e-473f-a460-c9fe7425f8a8","timestamp":"2026-05-21T14:25:06.997Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01T9gXgu2YKatNRVv6oKwA3A","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01X9EkUtMXANqBevXG1jbZBV","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 ARR submission. Your focus is **novelty and related work positioning**.\n\n## Venue Calibration\nEMNLP 2026 ARR Overall Assessment scale (1-5, half-point):\n- 5 = Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Conference acceptance\n- 3 = Findings acceptance\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\nAcceptance rate ~21%. Be calibrated accordingly.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"Detecting Architecture-Dependent Style Bias in LLM Judges via Controlled Style Decomposition\"\n\nThe paper introduces CSD (Controlled Style Decomposition), a 3-stage black-box framework for per-judge, per-axis style bias detection in LLM judges:\n1. Counterfactual generation: style-manipulated pairs along isolated axes (formality, verbosity, register)\n2. NLI-based content gating: bidirectional entailment filtering\n3. Position-controlled pairwise evaluation + Bradley-Terry modeling\n\nKey findings:\n- Architecture-dependent bias: 2/4 judges (GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B) show significant style bias; 2/4 (Claude, Llama) show none\n- \"Likert Blindspot\": 83% of biased cells detected only by pairwise comparison\n- Amplification is architecture-dependent (8-11pp biased judges, 2-4pp unbiased)\n- Conservative corrected estimates throughout\n- Cross-benchmark consistency, rewriter independence\n\n## Related Work as presented in paper (§2):\nThree paragraphs:\n1. \"Style bias in LLM evaluation\" — cites SOSBench, unnamed verbose/formal preference studies. Claims prior work diagnoses at benchmark level, not individual judge level, and none provide per-axis decomposition with content control.\n2. \"Pairwise vs. Likert evaluation\" — cites Jeong et al. \"Comparative Trap.\" Claims complementary finding: amplification is architecture-dependent.\n3. \"Content-controlled evaluation\" — cites RATE (ATE for style bias but Likert-based), CASSE (multi-dimensional but correlational), LC-AlpacaEval (single dimension). Claims CSD is first to combine NLI-gated content control + per-axis decomposition + pairwise comparison.\n\n**NOTE**: All citations in the PDF appear as \"(?)\" or \"(??)\" — meaning the bibliography is missing or broken. This is a significant formatting issue.\n\n## Contributions claimed:\nC1: CSD framework (methodology) — first per-judge, per-axis bias profiling tool\nC2: Architecture-dependent bias finding — 2/4 split, judge-specific not format-universal\nC3: Likert Blindspot formalization — 83% of biased cells missed by Likert\nC4: Honest amplification accounting — placebo-controlled, conservative correction\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed R2 review focusing on:\n\n1. **Novelty assessment**: How novel is CSD compared to RATE, CASSE, Comparative Trap? Is the combination of existing techniques sufficient for a novelty claim? Is \"architecture-dependent bias\" a genuinely new finding?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are important baselines or prior works missing? Is the positioning fair?\n3. **Contribution significance**: Are the claims appropriately scoped? Is the 2/4 split sufficient to claim \"architecture-dependent\"? \n4. **Narrative coherence**: Does the story hold together? Is \"Likert Blindspot\" well-motivated?\n5. **Writing quality and presentation**: Is the paper well-written? Tables clear? Any formatting issues?\n6. **Missing citations issue**: The bibliography appears entirely broken (all \"?\" references)\n\nConsider these specific questions:\n- Is combining NLI gating + pairwise + per-axis decomposition genuinely novel, or is it incremental engineering?\n- With only 4 judges (2 biased, 2 not), can the paper credibly claim \"architecture-dependent\" patterns? Or is this just \"some judges are biased, some aren't\"?\n- The \"Likert Blindspot\" framing — is this a genuine measurement insight or a predictable consequence of Likert's known lower sensitivity?\n- How does this compare to concurrent work on style bias (e.g., Koo et al. 2023 \"Benchmarking Cognitive Biases in LLM-as-Judge\", Wu et al. 2024 \"Style Over Substance\")?\n\nProvide scores for:\n- Excitement (1-5) — how novel/impactful is this?\n- Soundness (1-5) — from a framing/claims perspective\n- Overall Assessment (1-5, half-point, calibrated to EMNLP acceptance rate)\n\nFormat your review as a structured EMNLP-style review with: Summary, Strengths, Weaknesses, Questions for Authors, Missing References, and Scores.","model":"opus"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":32126,"cache_read_input_tokens":23947,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":32126},"output_tokens":25,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"05b18906-f618-42bb-ba5a-27a6b0fbf38c","uuid":"359378b4-ed43-4584-b7cd-feb91307a915"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"af1a0526cf97c4937","tool_use_id":"toolu_01X9EkUtMXANqBevXG1jbZBV","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 ARR submission. Your focus is **novelty and related work positioning**.\n\n## Venue Calibration\nEMNLP 2026 ARR Overall Assessment scale (1-5, half-point):\n- 5 = Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Conference acceptance\n- 3 = Findings acceptance\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\nAcceptance rate ~21%. Be calibrated accordingly.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"Detecting Architecture-Dependent Style Bias in LLM Judges via Controlled Style Decomposition\"\n\nThe paper introduces CSD (Controlled Style Decomposition), a 3-stage black-box framework for per-judge, per-axis style bias detection in LLM judges:\n1. Counterfactual generation: style-manipulated pairs along isolated axes (formality, verbosity, register)\n2. NLI-based content gating: bidirectional entailment filtering\n3. Position-controlled pairwise evaluation + Bradley-Terry modeling\n\nKey findings:\n- Architecture-dependent bias: 2/4 judges (GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B) show significant style bias; 2/4 (Claude, Llama) show none\n- \"Likert Blindspot\": 83% of biased cells detected only by pairwise comparison\n- Amplification is architecture-dependent (8-11pp biased judges, 2-4pp unbiased)\n- Conservative corrected estimates throughout\n- Cross-benchmark consistency, rewriter independence\n\n## Related Work as presented in paper (§2):\nThree paragraphs:\n1. \"Style bias in LLM evaluation\" — cites SOSBench, unnamed verbose/formal preference studies. Claims prior work diagnoses at benchmark level, not individual judge level, and none provide per-axis decomposition with content control.\n2. \"Pairwise vs. Likert evaluation\" — cites Jeong et al. \"Comparative Trap.\" Claims complementary finding: amplification is architecture-dependent.\n3. \"Content-controlled evaluation\" — cites RATE (ATE for style bias but Likert-based), CASSE (multi-dimensional but correlational), LC-AlpacaEval (single dimension). Claims CSD is first to combine NLI-gated content control + per-axis decomposition + pairwise comparison.\n\n**NOTE**: All citations in the PDF appear as \"(?)\" or \"(??)\" — meaning the bibliography is missing or broken. This is a significant formatting issue.\n\n## Contributions claimed:\nC1: CSD framework (methodology) — first per-judge, per-axis bias profiling tool\nC2: Architecture-dependent bias finding — 2/4 split, judge-specific not format-universal\nC3: Likert Blindspot formalization — 83% of biased cells missed by Likert\nC4: Honest amplification accounting — placebo-controlled, conservative correction\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed R2 review focusing on:\n\n1. **Novelty assessment**: How novel is CSD compared to RATE, CASSE, Comparative Trap? Is the combination of existing techniques sufficient for a novelty claim? Is \"architecture-dependent bias\" a genuinely new finding?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are important baselines or prior works missing? Is the positioning fair?\n3. **Contribution significance**: Are the claims appropriately scoped? Is the 2/4 split sufficient to claim \"architecture-dependent\"? \n4. **Narrative coherence**: Does the story hold together? Is \"Likert Blindspot\" well-motivated?\n5. **Writing quality and presentation**: Is the paper well-written? Tables clear? Any formatting issues?\n6. **Missing citations issue**: The bibliography appears entirely broken (all \"?\" references)\n\nConsider these specific questions:\n- Is combining NLI gating + pairwise + per-axis decomposition genuinely novel, or is it incremental engineering?\n- With only 4 judges (2 biased, 2 not), can the paper credibly claim \"architecture-dependent\" patterns? Or is this just \"some judges are biased, some aren't\"?\n- The \"Likert Blindspot\" framing — is this a genuine measurement insight or a predictable consequence of Likert's known lower sensitivity?\n- How does this compare to concurrent work on style bias (e.g., Koo et al. 2023 \"Benchmarking Cognitive Biases in LLM-as-Judge\", Wu et al. 2024 \"Style Over Substance\")?\n\nProvide scores for:\n- Excitement (1-5) — how novel/impactful is this?\n- Soundness (1-5) — from a framing/claims perspective\n- Overall Assessment (1-5, half-point, calibrated to EMNLP acceptance rate)\n\nFormat your review as a structured EMNLP-style review with: Summary, Strengths, Weaknesses, Questions for Authors, Missing References, and Scores.","uuid":"88f97ca7-974a-4a72-ab7f-1ebaaa677de5","session_id":"05b18906-f618-42bb-ba5a-27a6b0fbf38c"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 ARR submission. Your focus is **novelty and related work positioning**.\n\n## Venue Calibration\nEMNLP 2026 ARR Overall Assessment scale (1-5, half-point):\n- 5 = Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Conference acceptance\n- 3 = Findings acceptance\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\nAcceptance rate ~21%. Be calibrated accordingly.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"Detecting Architecture-Dependent Style Bias in LLM Judges via Controlled Style Decomposition\"\n\nThe paper introduces CSD (Controlled Style Decomposition), a 3-stage black-box framework for per-judge, per-axis style bias detection in LLM judges:\n1. Counterfactual generation: style-manipulated pairs along isolated axes (formality, verbosity, register)\n2. NLI-based content gating: bidirectional entailment filtering\n3. Position-controlled pairwise evaluation + Bradley-Terry modeling\n\nKey findings:\n- Architecture-dependent bias: 2/4 judges (GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B) show significant style bias; 2/4 (Claude, Llama) show none\n- \"Likert Blindspot\": 83% of biased cells detected only by pairwise comparison\n- Amplification is architecture-dependent (8-11pp biased judges, 2-4pp unbiased)\n- Conservative corrected estimates throughout\n- Cross-benchmark consistency, rewriter independence\n\n## Related Work as presented in paper (§2):\nThree paragraphs:\n1. \"Style bias in LLM evaluation\" — cites SOSBench, unnamed verbose/formal preference studies. Claims prior work diagnoses at benchmark level, not individual judge level, and none provide per-axis decomposition with content control.\n2. \"Pairwise vs. Likert evaluation\" — cites Jeong et al. \"Comparative Trap.\" Claims complementary finding: amplification is architecture-dependent.\n3. \"Content-controlled evaluation\" — cites RATE (ATE for style bias but Likert-based), CASSE (multi-dimensional but correlational), LC-AlpacaEval (single dimension). Claims CSD is first to combine NLI-gated content control + per-axis decomposition + pairwise comparison.\n\n**NOTE**: All citations in the PDF appear as \"(?)\" or \"(??)\" — meaning the bibliography is missing or broken. This is a significant formatting issue.\n\n## Contributions claimed:\nC1: CSD framework (methodology) — first per-judge, per-axis bias profiling tool\nC2: Architecture-dependent bias finding — 2/4 split, judge-specific not format-universal\nC3: Likert Blindspot formalization — 83% of biased cells missed by Likert\nC4: Honest amplification accounting — placebo-controlled, conservative correction\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed R2 review focusing on:\n\n1. **Novelty assessment**: How novel is CSD compared to RATE, CASSE, Comparative Trap? Is the combination of existing techniques sufficient for a novelty claim? Is \"architecture-dependent bias\" a genuinely new finding?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are important baselines or prior works missing? Is the positioning fair?\n3. **Contribution significance**: Are the claims appropriately scoped? Is the 2/4 split sufficient to claim \"architecture-dependent\"? \n4. **Narrative coherence**: Does the story hold together? Is \"Likert Blindspot\" well-motivated?\n5. **Writing quality and presentation**: Is the paper well-written? Tables clear? Any formatting issues?\n6. **Missing citations issue**: The bibliography appears entirely broken (all \"?\" references)\n\nConsider these specific questions:\n- Is combining NLI gating + pairwise + per-axis decomposition genuinely novel, or is it incremental engineering?\n- With only 4 judges (2 biased, 2 not), can the paper credibly claim \"architecture-dependent\" patterns? Or is this just \"some judges are biased, some aren't\"?\n- The \"Likert Blindspot\" framing — is this a genuine measurement insight or a predictable consequence of Likert's known lower sensitivity?\n- How does this compare to concurrent work on style bias (e.g., Koo et al. 2023 \"Benchmarking Cognitive Biases in LLM-as-Judge\", Wu et al. 2024 \"Style Over Substance\")?\n\nProvide scores for:\n- Excitement (1-5) — how novel/impactful is this?\n- Soundness (1-5) — from a framing/claims perspective\n- Overall Assessment (1-5, half-point, calibrated to EMNLP acceptance rate)\n\nFormat your review as a structured EMNLP-style review with: Summary, Strengths, Weaknesses, Questions for Authors, Missing References, and Scores."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01X9EkUtMXANqBevXG1jbZBV","session_id":"05b18906-f618-42bb-ba5a-27a6b0fbf38c","uuid":"58fb884c-7f3f-4c8d-8d40-3d9354a05a52","timestamp":"2026-05-21T14:25:07.442Z"}
