{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ViRE7xK1dF6swDmGEDtGe7","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n2\t\n3\t\\usepackage[review]{acl}\n4\t\n5\t\\usepackage{times}\n6\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n7\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n8\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n9\t\\usepackage{microtype}\n10\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n11\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n12\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n13\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n14\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n15\t\\usepackage{array}\n16\t\n17\t\\title{The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias\\\\in LLM Judges via Controlled Style Decomposition}\n18\t\n19\t\\author{Anonymous}\n20\t\n21\t\\begin{document}\n22\t\\maketitle\n23\t\n24\t\\begin{abstract}\n25\tLLM-as-judge evaluation is increasingly central to NLP benchmarking and alignment, yet style biases---systematic preferences for superficial textual properties like verbosity or formality---can silently corrupt scores.\n26\tWe introduce \\textbf{Controlled Style Decomposition (CSD)}, a black-box auditing framework that combines NLI-gated counterfactual rewriting, per-axis style isolation, and position-controlled pairwise comparison to detect and decompose style biases in LLM judges.\n27\tAcross three style axes (verbosity, formality, register), four judge families (GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B, Llama-3.3-70B, Gemini-2.0-Flash), and three benchmarks (LLMBar, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench), CSD reveals a \\textbf{Likert Blindspot}: pairwise Bradley-Terry estimation detects significant style bias (BT 63--77\\%, $p < 0.003$) in 12 of 18 judge$\\times$axis$\\times$benchmark cells where Likert scoring is non-significant in 10 of those 12 (sensitivity ratio $6{\\times}$).\n28\tGEE decomposition further reveals per-axis confound structure: verbosity is a pure style bias (Style OR $\\approx 11$--13, Originality OR non-significant), while formality is a conditional effect moderated by content quality changes.\n29\tDouble-rewrite placebo controls (BT $\\approx 50\\%$) rule out pairwise format amplification.\n30\tClaude Sonnet~4, tested identically, shows no bias (BT 54\\%, $p > 0.09$), confirming CSD detects real variation rather than methodological artifacts.\n31\t\\end{abstract}\n32\t\n33\t\\section{Introduction}\n34\t\\label{sec:intro}\n35\t\n36\tLLM-as-judge evaluation has become the de facto standard for comparing language model outputs \\citep{zheng2023judging,dubois2024alpacafarm}, powering major benchmarks like Chatbot Arena, AlpacaEval, and MT-Bench.\n37\tHowever, mounting evidence shows that LLM judges exhibit systematic biases---preferring verbose, formal, or well-structured outputs regardless of substantive quality \\citep{wu2024style,park2024offsetbias,stureborg2024large}.\n38\tStyle bias is not merely an academic concern: \\citet{koo2023benchmarking} find it is the dominant driver of score variation (SOS-Bench $R = 0.999$).\n39\t\n40\tThe standard approach to detecting such bias uses Likert-scale scoring---asking a judge to rate individual responses on a numeric scale and comparing mean ratings between style-manipulated variants.\n41\tWe demonstrate that this approach systematically fails.\n42\tUsing counterfactual pairs that differ only in style (with content preservation verified by NLI), we show that pairwise comparison detects significant bias (Bradley-Terry $P(\\text{styled}) = 63$--$77\\%$) in settings where Likert scoring yields non-significant effects ($p > 0.15$, $d < 0.06$).\n43\tWe term this the \\textbf{Likert Blindspot}: a measurement attenuation phenomenon where tie-rate compression in absolute scoring obscures biases that ordinal comparison readily reveals.\n44\t\n45\tOur contributions are:\n46\t\n47\t\\begin{enumerate}\n48\t\\item \\textbf{CSD framework} (\\S\\ref{sec:method}): A pipeline combining NLI-gated counterfactual rewriting, per-axis style decomposition, position-controlled pairwise comparison, and GEE-based confound separation for auditing black-box LLM judges.\n49\t\n50\t\\item \\textbf{Likert Blindspot} (\\S\\ref{sec:blindspot}): Systematic evidence that Likert scoring fails to detect style bias in 10 of 12 cases where pairwise BT is significant---a $6\\times$ sensitivity gap. The attenuation threshold is approximately BT $> 80\\%$.\n51\t\n52\t\\item \\textbf{Per-axis bias taxonomy} (\\S\\ref{sec:gee}): GEE decomposition reveals that verbosity is a confound-free style bias, formality is a conditional effect (style$+$originality), and register is originality-dominated---a structure stable across three independent judge families.\n53\t\n54\t\\item \\textbf{Practitioner guidance} (\\S\\ref{sec:discussion}): Content-controlled pairwise comparison with NLI gating should be the default for style bias auditing; per-axis decomposition reveals axis-specific confound structure invisible to aggregate measures.\n55\t\\end{enumerate}\n56\t\n57\t\\section{Related Work}\n58\t\\label{sec:related}\n59\t\n60\t\\paragraph{LLM-as-judge biases.}\n61\t\\citet{zheng2023judging} identify position, verbosity, and self-enhancement biases in MT-Bench.\n62\t\\citet{wu2024style} and \\citet{park2024offsetbias} demonstrate style-content confounding in evaluation.\n63\t\\citet{stureborg2024large} systematically catalog biases including anchoring, sycophancy, and formatting preferences.\n64\t\\citet{koo2023benchmarking} show style factors dominate score variation via structural equation modeling.\n65\tMost prior work reports biases using Likert-scale comparisons; we show this methodology systematically underestimates bias magnitude.\n66\t\n67\t\\paragraph{Measurement methodology.}\n68\tRATE \\citep{ye2024beyond} proposes Likert-based average treatment effects for LLM bias quantification.\n69\tCASSE \\citep{chen2024casse} decomposes evaluation into multiple style dimensions but uses correlational analysis.\n70\tLC-AlpacaEval \\citep{dubois2024length} controls for length as a single dimension.\n71\tCSD differs by combining NLI-gated content control with per-axis decomposition and explicit comparison of Likert versus pairwise detection power.\n72\tThe classical insight that ordinal comparison is more sensitive than absolute rating dates to \\citet{thurstone1927law} and \\citet{stevens1946theory}; we provide the first systematic evidence of this phenomenon in LLM evaluation.\n73\t\n74\t\\paragraph{Pairwise format considerations.}\n75\t\\citet{jeong2025comparative} identify a ``Comparative Trap'' where pairwise comparison can amplify small differences.\n76\tWe directly address this concern with double-rewrite placebo controls (\\S\\ref{sec:placebo}), NLI gate ablation (\\S\\ref{sec:nli_ablation}), and a bias-free negative control judge (\\S\\ref{sec:negative_control}).\n77\t\n78\t\\section{Method: Controlled Style Decomposition}\n79\t\\label{sec:method}\n80\t\n81\tCSD operates in four stages (Figure~\\ref{fig:pipeline}).\n82\t\n83\t\\paragraph{Stage 1: Counterfactual generation.}\n84\tGiven a source text $x$, we generate a style-manipulated variant $x'$ along a single axis $a \\in \\{\\text{verbosity, formality, register}\\}$ using an LLM rewriter.\n85\tThe rewriting prompt instructs the model to change \\emph{only} the target style property while preserving content, meaning, and quality.\n86\t\n87\t\\paragraph{Stage 2: NLI gating.}\n88\tEach pair $(x, x')$ is passed through a natural language inference model that verifies bidirectional entailment: $\\text{NLI}(x \\rightarrow x') = \\textsc{entailment}$ and $\\text{NLI}(x' \\rightarrow x) = \\textsc{entailment}$.\n89\tPairs failing either direction are excluded.\n90\tThis filters out rewrites that alter semantic content, restricting the manipulation to surface style.\n91\tThe NLI gate achieves 88\\% pass rate for formality and 86\\% for verbosity, with cross-validation precision of 92.1\\% and 82.6\\% respectively (GPT-4o on 200 pairs).\n92\t\n93\t\\paragraph{Stage 3: Position-controlled pairwise comparison.}\n94\tEach NLI-verified pair is presented to the target judge LLM in both orders $(x, x')$ and $(x', x)$, yielding two binary preference judgments per pair.\n95\tPosition effects are canceled by aggregation.\n96\tWe also collect Likert ratings for the same pairs by presenting each text individually and requesting a quality score.\n97\t\n98\t\\paragraph{Stage 4: Statistical analysis.}\n99\t\\emph{Bradley-Terry estimation}: pair-level bootstrap (10{,}000 iterations) estimates $P(\\text{styled wins})$ with 95\\% CIs.\n100\t\\emph{Likert ATE}: paired $t$-test on rating differences with Cohen's $d$ effect size."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_015zPKBUJH2eqBWZ6KDNPejT","session_id":"05b18906-f618-42bb-ba5a-27a6b0fbf38c","uuid":"628d06de-e672-4a88-babb-343a6a239c20","timestamp":"2026-05-21T14:25:24.924Z"}
