{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Co3g5wtZMAjR2MEHtB8hhJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KGtvn3YoCXzvUdE9sSB3Ya","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_5/projects/causal_style_decomp_judge/docs/paper/sections/introduction.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4072,"cache_read_input_tokens":25845,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4072,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01X9EkUtMXANqBevXG1jbZBV","session_id":"05b18906-f618-42bb-ba5a-27a6b0fbf38c","uuid":"f667cd89-0fa1-4780-b091-b3f4dc1933a4"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KGtvn3YoCXzvUdE9sSB3Ya","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Introduction}\n2\t\n3\tLLM judges are central infrastructure for NLP evaluation (AlpacaEval, MT-Bench, Chatbot Arena) and alignment training (RLHF, DPO), where judge-generated preferences serve as reward signals.\n4\tAny systematic bias in their assessments propagates through both evaluation leaderboards and model development pipelines.\n5\t\n6\tRecent work suggests that style features alone explain most of the variance in judge scores \\citep{murugadoss2025sos}, motivating systematic measurement of per-axis style bias.\n7\t\\citet{li2026preference} demonstrate that 76--92\\% of observed preference signals originate from surface-level style cues rather than substantive quality differences, and \\citet{soumik2026judging} report bias scores of 0.76--0.92 across five different judges.\n8\tDespite this consensus on severity, existing approaches each address individual requirements: CASSE \\citep{casse2026concise} and FairJudge \\citep{wang2026fairjudge} provide multi-dimensional style analysis, RATE \\citep{reber2025rate} introduces interventional design, and length-controlled AlpacaEval \\citep{dubois2025lc} offers population-level correction.\n9\tHowever, no single framework integrates all four requirements---per-axis decomposition, interventional identification, per-instance diagnostics, and black-box applicability.\n10\tA further concern compounds these limitations: standard Likert scoring suffers from a measurement efficiency gap---integer discretization produces ${\\sim}60\\%$ uninformative ties, and even non-tied pairs carry weaker signal than pairwise comparison---a limitation predicted by measurement theory \\citep{thurstone1927law} but previously unquantified for LLM judges.\n11\t\n12\tWe propose Controlled Style Decomposition (CSD), the first framework combining NLI-gated content control, per-axis style decomposition, and position-controlled pairwise comparison to systematically quantify style bias in LLM judges.\n13\tInspired by interventional approaches to text analysis \\citep{veitch2020adapting}, CSD operates in three stages: binary style-controlled rewriting along a single axis (e.g., formal vs.\\ casual), content preservation verification through Natural Language Inference (NLI) entailment scoring, and bias measurement via position-controlled pairwise comparison.\n14\tBecause CSD requires no access to judge internals and applies post-hoc to any black-box judge, it complements existing population-level approaches with finer-grained, per-instance diagnostics.\n15\tOur experiments quantify this efficiency gap---which we term the \\emph{Likert Blindspot}---across two target judges (GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B) and one negative control (Claude Sonnet 4), three benchmarks, and two primary style axes (formality, verbosity), with exploratory evidence on register.\n16\tOn the same formality data, Likert scoring yields non-significant ATE ($p = 0.332$, $p = 0.908$) for both judges, while Bradley-Terry analysis detects 63.0\\% (GPT-4o, $p = 0.0004$) and 66.5\\% (Qwen3-32B, $p < 0.0001$) formal win probabilities; verbosity shows 77.3\\% BT ($p < 0.0001$) invisible to Likert ($p = 0.179$; Figure~\\ref{fig:forest_plot}).\n17\t\n18\tOur contributions are as follows:\n19\t\\begin{itemize}\n20\t    \\item \\textbf{A black-box interventional framework} for detecting per-axis style bias in LLM judges through style-controlled rewriting and position-controlled pairwise comparison (Section~\\ref{sec:method}).\n21\t    \\item \\textbf{The Likert Blindspot as measurement attenuation:} Likert scoring attenuates style bias along a severity spectrum---completely at moderate bias ($d \\to 0$ at $n = 355$), by ${>}60\\%$ at stronger effects---not merely a power gap; across four judge$\\times$axis configurations at $p < 0.001$, pairwise comparison detects bias that Likert misses (all $p > 0.17$). Seven statistical methods confirm the effect is structural (Section~\\ref{sec:analysis}, Tables~\\ref{tab:main_results}--\\ref{tab:sensitivity}).\n22\t    \\item \\textbf{Per-axis confound structure:} Verbosity bias shows no observed originality confound, while formality and register show conditional style effects alongside significant originality confounds---a two-tier structure replicating across judges (Section~\\ref{sec:direction}, Table~\\ref{tab:gee}).\n23\t    \\item \\textbf{Practical implications:} in typical evaluation settings ($n < 500$), style bias audits should employ pairwise comparison and per-axis analysis; existing Likert-based findings may systematically underestimate bias magnitude (Section~\\ref{sec:conclusion}).\n24\t\\end{itemize}\n25\t\n26\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n27\t\\centering\n28\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/pipeline_diagram.pdf}\n29\t\\caption{CSD pipeline overview. Numbers indicate sample attrition at each stage.}\n30\t\\label{fig:pipeline}\n31\t\\end{figure}\n32\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01X9EkUtMXANqBevXG1jbZBV","session_id":"05b18906-f618-42bb-ba5a-27a6b0fbf38c","uuid":"93791d11-d813-468f-8046-124ad366cb09","timestamp":"2026-05-21T14:25:30.994Z"}
