{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015zPKBUJH2eqBWZ6KDNPejT","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of the paper, its experimental setup, data, and internal project context. Let me compile my detailed R1 review.\n\n---\n\n## EMNLP 2026 ARR Review — Reviewer 1 (Methods & Experiments Focus)\n\n**Paper**: \"The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Controlled Style Decomposition\"\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper presents Controlled Style Decomposition (CSD), a three-stage black-box framework for detecting per-axis style bias in LLM judges. The pipeline generates counterfactual text pairs manipulated along a single style axis (formality, verbosity, register), filters content-contaminated pairs via NLI entailment gating, and measures judge bias through position-controlled pairwise comparison with Bradley-Terry modeling. The central empirical finding is the \"Likert Blindspot\": pairwise comparison detects significant style bias (BT 63-77%) in configurations where all tested Likert-based methods produce non-significant results. GEE conditional decomposition separates style effects from originality confounds, revealing a per-axis taxonomy: verbosity is a clean style bias, formality is a conditional effect with originality confounding, and register is originality-dominated. Experiments span two biased judges (GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B), one negative-control judge (Claude Sonnet 4), three benchmarks (LLMBar, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench), and include rewriter-independence controls, NLI gate ablation, and scaled replication.\n\n---\n\n### Strengths\n\n**S1. Methodologically careful pipeline design.** The three-stage CSD pipeline is well-motivated and addresses real gaps in the style bias detection literature. The combination of NLI-gated content control, per-axis decomposition, and position-controlled pairwise comparison is a principled integration of existing techniques that no prior work has assembled. The DAG in Figure 2 honestly represents the assumed data-generating process and explicitly marks residual confounding paths rather than overclaiming causal identification.\n\n**S2. Exceptional statistical rigor.** The paper demonstrates unusually thorough statistical methodology for an NLP venue: Holm-Bonferroni family-wise correction for the primary 2x3 grid, clear separation of exploratory vs. confirmatory analyses, seven independent statistical methods applied to the same data to confirm the Blindspot is structural, BT goodness-of-fit testing, GEE with correlation-structure robustness checks (exchangeable vs. independence), interaction tests, TOST equivalence testing for the negative control, and bootstrap CIs throughout. The pre-specified primary analysis family is commendable.\n\n**S3. Honest treatment of confounds and limitations.** The paper does not hide weaknesses. The direction asymmetry in formality, the originality confound, the evaluator-producer overlap (Qwen3-32B as both rewriter and judge), and the fundamental identifiability limit of text-rewriting approaches are all explicitly discussed. The GEE decomposition revealing a two-tier confound structure (clean verbosity vs. confounded formality) is genuinely informative rather than swept under the rug.\n\n**S4. Strong control experiments.** The Claude Sonnet 4 negative control (same pipeline, no bias detected) is the single most effective piece of evidence that the pipeline does not fabricate bias. The NLI gate ablation showing formality-fail pairs reverse to 41.7% (preferring casual) is compelling evidence that the gate removes content-contaminated pairs rather than amplifying style signal. The rewriter-independence test with GPT-4o as rewriter (Delta < 3.8pp) bounds evaluator-producer effects.\n\n**S5. The Blindspot finding is practically significant.** The demonstration that seven Likert variants all fail to detect bias that pairwise comparison reliably detects, confirmed at n=355 where Likert d converges to ~0, has real implications for the evaluation community. This is not merely a power argument; it is measurement attenuation.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n**W1. Small primary sample sizes with limited statistical power for secondary analyses (Major).**\nThe core experiments use LLMBar (n=100 raw, ~88 after NLI filtering). While adequate for the primary BT tests (which achieve significance), this sample size is marginal for the GEE decomposition. The formality GEE at n=88 produces an OR of 4.20 for Qwen3-32B that drops to 2.15 at n=355, a nearly 2x reduction that the authors themselves attribute to small-sample upward bias. This suggests the n=88 estimates for other cells (register, GPT-4o formality) may also be inflated. The NLI-fail subset for the ablation analysis contains only ~12 pairs, which the authors acknowledge but still draw directional conclusions from. The register analysis (GPT-4o BT 57.0%, p=0.04; Qwen3-32B 55.3%, p=0.076) is underpowered and should not be presented alongside the well-powered verbosity and formality results without stronger caveats.\n\n**W2. The \"Likert Blindspot\" framing conflates measurement sensitivity with construct validity (Major).**\nThe paper acknowledges in one sentence that \"an equally plausible interpretation is that Likert and pairwise modes measure fundamentally different constructs\" but then proceeds to frame the entire paper as if the pairwise result is the ground truth and Likert is deficient. If Likert measures absolute quality perception and pairwise measures relative ordinal preference, then the \"Blindspot\" may simply reflect that style does not meaningfully affect absolute quality perception---a potentially positive finding about judge robustness to style in pointwise settings. The paper needs to take this alternative interpretation more seriously, especially given that Jeong et al. (2025) provide evidence that pairwise comparison amplifies micro-preferences. The four-point defense against the Comparative Trap (Section 5.2) is reasonable but not fully dispositive: Claude's null result could reflect model-specific robustness rather than ruling out format amplification for other models; the cross-axis gradient is consistent with both amplification and genuine bias variation; and the debiased prompt reducing Qwen3-32B by 5.7pp but not eliminating bias is ambiguous.\n\n**W3. Only two target judges show bias; generalizability is limited (Major).**\nGPT-4o and Qwen3-32B show bias; Claude Sonnet 4 does not. With only two positive and one negative example, the paper cannot substantiate claims about \"architecture-dependent\" bias patterns. The title in the submitted version (\"The Likert Blindspot\") is more appropriate than the earlier title (\"Detecting Architecture-Dependent Style Bias\"), but the 2-vs-1 split is still thin evidence for broad claims about the evaluation community needing to adopt pairwise methods. The roadmap indicates Llama-3.3-70B experiments were in progress but not completed; their inclusion would have meaningfully strengthened the paper.\n\n**W4. NLI gate validation relies on LLM annotators, not human evaluation (Moderate).**\nThe NLI gate is validated against Qwen3-32B as LLM annotator (91/100 agreement) and GPT-4o cross-validation (92.1%/82.6% precision). No human evaluation of content preservation is performed. For a paper whose central methodological claim rests on the NLI gate successfully isolating style from content, human validation of a sample of NLI decisions would substantially strengthen the evidence. The direction-aware gating for verbosity admits 21 additional pairs with lower precision (82.6% vs. 92.1%), and the paper's own Table on rewriting artifacts shows judges strongly prefer LLM-polished text (verbosity: original win rate 0-6.2%), raising the question of whether the verbosity \"style bias\" partially reflects preference for LLM-generated text quality rather than verbosity per se.\n\n**W5. The Equation 1 / BT correction is implicit rather than explicit (Moderate).**\nThe paper describes using Bradley-Terry modeling with the choix library but never writes out the correction formula that adjusts raw BT estimates for the double-rewrite placebo artifact. The GEE decomposition is described as controlling for the artifact, but the relationship between the raw BT numbers in Table 1 and the \"corrected\" estimates referenced in the paper summary is unclear. Are the BT numbers in Table 1 raw or corrected? If raw, what are the corrected numbers? The paper references \"conservative correction\" in the abstract summary but the main text appears to report raw BT throughout, with GEE as a separate analysis. This creates confusion about what exactly the headline effect sizes represent.\n\n**W6. Rewriter-judge overlap for the primary Qwen3-32B results (Moderate).**\nThe rewriter-independence test (Appendix D) shows the verbosity Style OR drops 22% (13.10 to 10.17) when switching from Qwen3-32B to GPT-4o as rewriter. While this bounds the self-preference inflation, a 22% decrease is non-trivial. More importantly, the formality rewriter-independence test only uses Qwen3-32B as judge---there is no test of whether GPT-4o as judge shows different results with different rewriters. The primary GPT-4o results use Qwen3-32B as rewriter throughout, and we cannot rule out that GPT-4o's formality bias is partly driven by preferring Qwen3-32B's rewriting style.\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **On the Blindspot interpretation**: If a practitioner uses pointwise Likert scoring for deployment decisions and pairwise comparison for auditing, what is the actionable implication when Likert shows no bias but pairwise does? Should they distrust the Likert scores used in production? Or does this suggest pointwise scoring is actually more robust to style for deployment purposes?\n\n2. **On the verbosity artifact**: The double-rewrite placebo shows 0% original win rate for verbosity (Qwen3-32B). This means the judge never prefers original text over its same-style rewrite for verbosity pairs. How do you separate \"preference for verbose text\" from \"preference for LLM-generated verbose text over human-written verbose text\" (i.e., a generation-quality confound)?\n\n3. **On GEE interpretation**: The formality Originality OR is 4.99 (p<0.0001) at n=355 for Qwen3-32B, substantially larger than the Style OR of 2.15. Doesn't this suggest that for formality, the dominant effect is originality preference rather than style preference? How should practitioners interpret a \"conditional style effect\" where the confound is larger than the effect of interest?\n\n4. **On the power curve (Figure 3)**: The power curves are computed at the observed effect sizes. Given that the n=88 estimates may be inflated (as demonstrated by the n=355 correction for Qwen3-32B formality), are these power curves calibrated to the true effect sizes or the inflated small-sample estimates?\n\n5. **On register**: Register enters the primary Holm-Bonferroni family (6 tests: 2 judges x 3 axes) but the paper also calls it \"exploratory\" in multiple places. Which is it? If it is exploratory, why is it in the primary family? If it is confirmatory, how do you justify a BT of 55.3% (p=0.076) as evidence of anything?\n\n---\n\n### Missing Experiments / Analyses\n\n1. **Human evaluation of NLI gate decisions**: Even a 50-pair human annotation of content preservation for both formality and verbosity pairs would substantially strengthen the core methodological claim. This is the single most impactful missing experiment.\n\n2. **Third target judge (Llama-3.3-70B)**: The roadmap confirms this was planned but not completed. With only 2/3 positive judges being from different families (one closed-source US, one open-source Chinese), a third architecture would address W3.\n\n3. **Rewriter independence for GPT-4o as judge**: The current rewriter-independence tests only use Qwen3-32B as judge. Testing whether GPT-4o judge results change with different rewriters would close a gap in the control matrix.\n\n4. **Longer-form text experiments**: All experiments use LLMBar (instruction-response pairs, relatively short). Style bias may behave differently on longer-form generation tasks. AlpacaEval and MT-Bench partially address this, but the paper would benefit from acknowledging this limitation more explicitly.\n\n5. **Effect of NLI threshold on the Blindspot classification**: The NLI threshold sensitivity analysis shows stable BT win rates. But does the Blindspot classification (pairwise significant, Likert not) change at different thresholds? This would address whether the Blindspot is robust to the specific NLI filtering decision.\n\n---\n\n### Missing References\n\n- Ye et al. (2024), \"Beyond Scalar Reward Model: Learning Generative Judge from Preference Data\" -- relevant to reward model bias detection methodology\n- Huang et al. (2024), \"Bias Benchmark for AI Judges\" -- directly relevant benchmark work\n- The paper cites 50 references, which is adequate for the scope.\n\n---\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n- The abstract mentions \"three benchmarks\" but the cross-benchmark analysis is incomplete: only formality is tested across all three benchmarks; verbosity is only on LLMBar and AlpacaEval; register is LLMBar only.\n- Table 1 footnotes are dense; consider moving some to a table note paragraph.\n- The paper is 6 pages of main text, well under the 8-page limit. The additional space could accommodate the human evaluation study or more detailed GEE diagnostics in the main text.\n- Section numbering jumps from \"Experiments\" (Section 4) to \"Analysis\" (Section 5) without clear demarcation of what constitutes primary results vs. robustness analyses.\n- The GEE CI table in the appendix has missing entries (GPT-4o verbosity, GPT-4o formality, GPT-4o register CIs are blank).\n\n---\n\n### Scores\n\n| Criterion | Score | Justification |\n|-----------|-------|---------------|\n| **Soundness** | 3.5/5 | Pipeline is well-designed with appropriate controls; the NLI gate without human validation is a gap; the Blindspot interpretation has a plausible alternative (construct difference vs. measurement failure) that is underexplored; small-sample GEE estimates may be inflated |\n| **Reproducibility** | 3.0/5 | Statistical methods are well-specified (library, seed, bootstrap count); but LLM API versions may change behavior, NLI threshold chosen without formal justification beyond bimodality argument, code/data not yet released, and some appendix CI entries are missing |\n| **Overall Assessment** | **3.5** (Borderline Conference) | The paper identifies a real and practically important phenomenon (Likert insensitivity to style bias detected by pairwise comparison), demonstrates it with thorough statistical methodology, and provides honest treatment of confounds. However, the small sample sizes, limited judge coverage (2 positive + 1 negative), absence of human evaluation for the NLI gate, and the unresolved construct-validity alternative for the Blindspot framing prevent confident acceptance at the main conference level. With the addition of a third target judge and human NLI validation, this would likely reach 4.0. As submitted, it is a strong Findings candidate or borderline main conference paper. |\n\n**Recommendation**: 3.5 -- Borderline Conference. The statistical methodology is among the most rigorous I have seen at this venue for this type of study, and the Blindspot finding has clear practical value. The main barriers to a higher score are (1) limited generalizability from only two biased judges, (2) the absence of human evaluation for the critical NLI content-preservation claim, and (3) the need for more careful engagement with the construct-validity alternative interpretation. If the authors can address these in revision (particularly items 1 and 2, which are empirical additions rather than conceptual changes), I would expect to raise my score."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a5ba0b2965c9435b1 (use SendMessage with to: 'a5ba0b2965c9435b1' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 65044\ntool_uses: 18\nduration_ms: 139392</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"05b18906-f618-42bb-ba5a-27a6b0fbf38c","uuid":"33b8e4bd-a673-4418-821e-0fa3a24960e6","timestamp":"2026-05-21T14:27:04.964Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an EMNLP 2026 ARR submission. Your focus is **methods and experiments**. \n\n## Venue Calibration\nEMNLP 2026 ARR Overall Assessment scale (1-5, half-point):\n- 5 = Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Conference acceptance\n- 3 = Findings acceptance\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\nAcceptance rate ~21%. Be calibrated accordingly.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"Detecting Architecture-Dependent Style Bias in LLM Judges via Controlled Style Decomposition\"\n\nThe paper introduces CSD (Controlled Style Decomposition), a 3-stage black-box framework for per-judge, per-axis style bias detection in LLM judges:\n1. Counterfactual generation: style-manipulated pairs along isolated axes (formality, verbosity, register)\n2. NLI-based content gating: bidirectional entailment filtering to remove content-contaminated pairs\n3. Position-controlled pairwise evaluation + Bradley-Terry modeling\n\nKey findings:\n- Architecture-dependent bias: 2/4 judges (GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B) show significant style bias; 2/4 (Claude Sonnet 4, Llama-3.3-70B) show none (confirmed by TOST equivalence tests)\n- \"Likert Blindspot\": 83% of biased cells detected only by pairwise, never by Likert (across 7 Likert variants)\n- Double-rewrite placebo controls show amplification is also architecture-dependent (8-11pp biased, 2-4pp unbiased)\n- After conservative correction: verbosity bias strong (65-68%), formality moderate-marginal (53-58%)\n- Cross-benchmark consistency (LLMBar, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench)\n- Rewriter independence (3 rewriter families, Δ < 3.8pp)\n- Scaled replication n=400 for one cell\n\nPaper is 6 pages of main text. No appendices visible beyond mention of Gemini in §5.6.\n\n## Tables in paper:\n- Table 1: 4-judge evidence matrix (LLMBar) - BT and Likert for formality+verbosity\n- Table 2: TOST equivalence testing (Δ=0.15)\n- Table 3: Blindspot classification (20 cells: 11 blindspot, 2 both sig, 7 both NS)\n- Table 4: Placebo BT and corrected style effects\n- Table 5: Cross-benchmark formality BT (3 benchmarks × 3 judges)\n- Table 6: Rewriter independence (3 rewriters × Qwen3-32B judge)\n- Table 7: Cross-axis gradient (Qwen3-32B, LLMBar)\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from context file)\nThe full experiment plan includes these experiments:\n1. exp-001: Multi-judge formality with Gemini addition (reported in §5.6)\n2. exp-002: Cross-benchmark formality for Llama + Qwen3-32B (Table 5 has Llama cross-benchmark; Claude missing)\n3. exp-003: Likert vs Pairwise sensitivity matrix (Table 3)\n4. exp-005: Double-rewrite placebo multi-judge (Table 4)\n5. exp-006: Scaled replication n=400 (§5.5)\n6. exp-009: Non-Qwen rewriter control (Table 6)\n7. exp-020: GPT-4o verbosity + register (Table 1 + §5.1)\n8. exp-021: Llama-3.3-70B third target judge (Tables 1-2)\n9. exp-022: Double-rewrite placebo focused (Table 4)\n10. exp-023: NLI gate ablation (§5.5)\n11. exp-024: Cross-axis gradient (Table 7)\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed R1 review focusing on:\n\n1. **Methodology soundness**: Is the CSD pipeline well-designed? Are there methodological gaps? Is the NLI gating sufficient? Is the BT correction formula (Eq. 1) valid?\n2. **Experimental rigor**: Sample sizes, statistical tests, multiple comparisons correction, effect sizes, confidence intervals\n3. **Controls and ablations**: Placebo controls, rewriter independence, NLI gate ablation, scaled replication — are they sufficient?\n4. **Missing experiments or analyses**: What's missing from what a careful reviewer would expect?\n5. **Reproducibility**: Can the experiments be reproduced from the description?\n6. **Specific weaknesses and strengths**\n\nProvide scores for:\n- Soundness (1-5)\n- Reproducibility (1-5)\n- Overall Assessment (1-5, half-point, calibrated to EMNLP acceptance rate)\n\nFormat your review as a structured EMNLP-style review with: Summary, Strengths, Weaknesses, Questions for Authors, Missing References/Experiments, and Scores.","agentId":"a5ba0b2965c9435b1","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of the paper, its experimental setup, data, and internal project context. Let me compile my detailed R1 review.\n\n---\n\n## EMNLP 2026 ARR Review — Reviewer 1 (Methods & Experiments Focus)\n\n**Paper**: \"The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Controlled Style Decomposition\"\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper presents Controlled Style Decomposition (CSD), a three-stage black-box framework for detecting per-axis style bias in LLM judges. The pipeline generates counterfactual text pairs manipulated along a single style axis (formality, verbosity, register), filters content-contaminated pairs via NLI entailment gating, and measures judge bias through position-controlled pairwise comparison with Bradley-Terry modeling. The central empirical finding is the \"Likert Blindspot\": pairwise comparison detects significant style bias (BT 63-77%) in configurations where all tested Likert-based methods produce non-significant results. GEE conditional decomposition separates style effects from originality confounds, revealing a per-axis taxonomy: verbosity is a clean style bias, formality is a conditional effect with originality confounding, and register is originality-dominated. Experiments span two biased judges (GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B), one negative-control judge (Claude Sonnet 4), three benchmarks (LLMBar, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench), and include rewriter-independence controls, NLI gate ablation, and scaled replication.\n\n---\n\n### Strengths\n\n**S1. Methodologically careful pipeline design.** The three-stage CSD pipeline is well-motivated and addresses real gaps in the style bias detection literature. The combination of NLI-gated content control, per-axis decomposition, and position-controlled pairwise comparison is a principled integration of existing techniques that no prior work has assembled. The DAG in Figure 2 honestly represents the assumed data-generating process and explicitly marks residual confounding paths rather than overclaiming causal identification.\n\n**S2. Exceptional statistical rigor.** The paper demonstrates unusually thorough statistical methodology for an NLP venue: Holm-Bonferroni family-wise correction for the primary 2x3 grid, clear separation of exploratory vs. confirmatory analyses, seven independent statistical methods applied to the same data to confirm the Blindspot is structural, BT goodness-of-fit testing, GEE with correlation-structure robustness checks (exchangeable vs. independence), interaction tests, TOST equivalence testing for the negative control, and bootstrap CIs throughout. The pre-specified primary analysis family is commendable.\n\n**S3. Honest treatment of confounds and limitations.** The paper does not hide weaknesses. The direction asymmetry in formality, the originality confound, the evaluator-producer overlap (Qwen3-32B as both rewriter and judge), and the fundamental identifiability limit of text-rewriting approaches are all explicitly discussed. The GEE decomposition revealing a two-tier confound structure (clean verbosity vs. confounded formality) is genuinely informative rather than swept under the rug.\n\n**S4. Strong control experiments.** The Claude Sonnet 4 negative control (same pipeline, no bias detected) is the single most effective piece of evidence that the pipeline does not fabricate bias. The NLI gate ablation showing formality-fail pairs reverse to 41.7% (preferring casual) is compelling evidence that the gate removes content-contaminated pairs rather than amplifying style signal. The rewriter-independence test with GPT-4o as rewriter (Delta < 3.8pp) bounds evaluator-producer effects.\n\n**S5. The Blindspot finding is practically significant.** The demonstration that seven Likert variants all fail to detect bias that pairwise comparison reliably detects, confirmed at n=355 where Likert d converges to ~0, has real implications for the evaluation community. This is not merely a power argument; it is measurement attenuation.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n**W1. Small primary sample sizes with limited statistical power for secondary analyses (Major).**\nThe core experiments use LLMBar (n=100 raw, ~88 after NLI filtering). While adequate for the primary BT tests (which achieve significance), this sample size is marginal for the GEE decomposition. The formality GEE at n=88 produces an OR of 4.20 for Qwen3-32B that drops to 2.15 at n=355, a nearly 2x reduction that the authors themselves attribute to small-sample upward bias. This suggests the n=88 estimates for other cells (register, GPT-4o formality) may also be inflated. The NLI-fail subset for the ablation analysis contains only ~12 pairs, which the authors acknowledge but still draw directional conclusions from. The register analysis (GPT-4o BT 57.0%, p=0.04; Qwen3-32B 55.3%, p=0.076) is underpowered and should not be presented alongside the well-powered verbosity and formality results without stronger caveats.\n\n**W2. The \"Likert Blindspot\" framing conflates measurement sensitivity with construct validity (Major).**\nThe paper acknowledges in one sentence that \"an equally plausible interpretation is that Likert and pairwise modes measure fundamentally different constructs\" but then proceeds to frame the entire paper as if the pairwise result is the ground truth and Likert is deficient. If Likert measures absolute quality perception and pairwise measures relative ordinal preference, then the \"Blindspot\" may simply reflect that style does not meaningfully affect absolute quality perception---a potentially positive finding about judge robustness to style in pointwise settings. The paper needs to take this alternative interpretation more seriously, especially given that Jeong et al. (2025) provide evidence that pairwise comparison amplifies micro-preferences. The four-point defense against the Comparative Trap (Section 5.2) is reasonable but not fully dispositive: Claude's null result could reflect model-specific robustness rather than ruling out format amplification for other models; the cross-axis gradient is consistent with both amplification and genuine bias variation; and the debiased prompt reducing Qwen3-32B by 5.7pp but not eliminating bias is ambiguous.\n\n**W3. Only two target judges show bias; generalizability is limited (Major).**\nGPT-4o and Qwen3-32B show bias; Claude Sonnet 4 does not. With only two positive and one negative example, the paper cannot substantiate claims about \"architecture-dependent\" bias patterns. The title in the submitted version (\"The Likert Blindspot\") is more appropriate than the earlier title (\"Detecting Architecture-Dependent Style Bias\"), but the 2-vs-1 split is still thin evidence for broad claims about the evaluation community needing to adopt pairwise methods. The roadmap indicates Llama-3.3-70B experiments were in progress but not completed; their inclusion would have meaningfully strengthened the paper.\n\n**W4. NLI gate validation relies on LLM annotators, not human evaluation (Moderate).**\nThe NLI gate is validated against Qwen3-32B as LLM annotator (91/100 agreement) and GPT-4o cross-validation (92.1%/82.6% precision). No human evaluation of content preservation is performed. For a paper whose central methodological claim rests on the NLI gate successfully isolating style from content, human validation of a sample of NLI decisions would substantially strengthen the evidence. The direction-aware gating for verbosity admits 21 additional pairs with lower precision (82.6% vs. 92.1%), and the paper's own Table on rewriting artifacts shows judges strongly prefer LLM-polished text (verbosity: original win rate 0-6.2%), raising the question of whether the verbosity \"style bias\" partially reflects preference for LLM-generated text quality rather than verbosity per se.\n\n**W5. The Equation 1 / BT correction is implicit rather than explicit (Moderate).**\nThe paper describes using Bradley-Terry modeling with the choix library but never writes out the correction formula that adjusts raw BT estimates for the double-rewrite placebo artifact. The GEE decomposition is described as controlling for the artifact, but the relationship between the raw BT numbers in Table 1 and the \"corrected\" estimates referenced in the paper summary is unclear. Are the BT numbers in Table 1 raw or corrected? If raw, what are the corrected numbers? The paper references \"conservative correction\" in the abstract summary but the main text appears to report raw BT throughout, with GEE as a separate analysis. This creates confusion about what exactly the headline effect sizes represent.\n\n**W6. Rewriter-judge overlap for the primary Qwen3-32B results (Moderate).**\nThe rewriter-independence test (Appendix D) shows the verbosity Style OR drops 22% (13.10 to 10.17) when switching from Qwen3-32B to GPT-4o as rewriter. While this bounds the self-preference inflation, a 22% decrease is non-trivial. More importantly, the formality rewriter-independence test only uses Qwen3-32B as judge---there is no test of whether GPT-4o as judge shows different results with different rewriters. The primary GPT-4o results use Qwen3-32B as rewriter throughout, and we cannot rule out that GPT-4o's formality bias is partly driven by preferring Qwen3-32B's rewriting style.\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **On the Blindspot interpretation**: If a practitioner uses pointwise Likert scoring for deployment decisions and pairwise comparison for auditing, what is the actionable implication when Likert shows no bias but pairwise does? Should they distrust the Likert scores used in production? Or does this suggest pointwise scoring is actually more robust to style for deployment purposes?\n\n2. **On the verbosity artifact**: The double-rewrite placebo shows 0% original win rate for verbosity (Qwen3-32B). This means the judge never prefers original text over its same-style rewrite for verbosity pairs. How do you separate \"preference for verbose text\" from \"preference for LLM-generated verbose text over human-written verbose text\" (i.e., a generation-quality confound)?\n\n3. **On GEE interpretation**: The formality Originality OR is 4.99 (p<0.0001) at n=355 for Qwen3-32B, substantially larger than the Style OR of 2.15. Doesn't this suggest that for formality, the dominant effect is originality preference rather than style preference? How should practitioners interpret a \"conditional style effect\" where the confound is larger than the effect of interest?\n\n4. **On the power curve (Figure 3)**: The power curves are computed at the observed effect sizes. Given that the n=88 estimates may be inflated (as demonstrated by the n=355 correction for Qwen3-32B formality), are these power curves calibrated to the true effect sizes or the inflated small-sample estimates?\n\n5. **On register**: Register enters the primary Holm-Bonferroni family (6 tests: 2 judges x 3 axes) but the paper also calls it \"exploratory\" in multiple places. Which is it? If it is exploratory, why is it in the primary family? If it is confirmatory, how do you justify a BT of 55.3% (p=0.076) as evidence of anything?\n\n---\n\n### Missing Experiments / Analyses\n\n1. **Human evaluation of NLI gate decisions**: Even a 50-pair human annotation of content preservation for both formality and verbosity pairs would substantially strengthen the core methodological claim. This is the single most impactful missing experiment.\n\n2. **Third target judge (Llama-3.3-70B)**: The roadmap confirms this was planned but not completed. With only 2/3 positive judges being from different families (one closed-source US, one open-source Chinese), a third architecture would address W3.\n\n3. **Rewriter independence for GPT-4o as judge**: The current rewriter-independence tests only use Qwen3-32B as judge. Testing whether GPT-4o judge results change with different rewriters would close a gap in the control matrix.\n\n4. **Longer-form text experiments**: All experiments use LLMBar (instruction-response pairs, relatively short). Style bias may behave differently on longer-form generation tasks. AlpacaEval and MT-Bench partially address this, but the paper would benefit from acknowledging this limitation more explicitly.\n\n5. **Effect of NLI threshold on the Blindspot classification**: The NLI threshold sensitivity analysis shows stable BT win rates. But does the Blindspot classification (pairwise significant, Likert not) change at different thresholds? This would address whether the Blindspot is robust to the specific NLI filtering decision.\n\n---\n\n### Missing References\n\n- Ye et al. (2024), \"Beyond Scalar Reward Model: Learning Generative Judge from Preference Data\" -- relevant to reward model bias detection methodology\n- Huang et al. (2024), \"Bias Benchmark for AI Judges\" -- directly relevant benchmark work\n- The paper cites 50 references, which is adequate for the scope.\n\n---\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n- The abstract mentions \"three benchmarks\" but the cross-benchmark analysis is incomplete: only formality is tested across all three benchmarks; verbosity is only on LLMBar and AlpacaEval; register is LLMBar only.\n- Table 1 footnotes are dense; consider moving some to a table note paragraph.\n- The paper is 6 pages of main text, well under the 8-page limit. The additional space could accommodate the human evaluation study or more detailed GEE diagnostics in the main text.\n- Section numbering jumps from \"Experiments\" (Section 4) to \"Analysis\" (Section 5) without clear demarcation of what constitutes primary results vs. robustness analyses.\n- The GEE CI table in the appendix has missing entries (GPT-4o verbosity, GPT-4o formality, GPT-4o register CIs are blank).\n\n---\n\n### Scores\n\n| Criterion | Score | Justification |\n|-----------|-------|---------------|\n| **Soundness** | 3.5/5 | Pipeline is well-designed with appropriate controls; the NLI gate without human validation is a gap; the Blindspot interpretation has a plausible alternative (construct difference vs. measurement failure) that is underexplored; small-sample GEE estimates may be inflated |\n| **Reproducibility** | 3.0/5 | Statistical methods are well-specified (library, seed, bootstrap count); but LLM API versions may change behavior, NLI threshold chosen without formal justification beyond bimodality argument, code/data not yet released, and some appendix CI entries are missing |\n| **Overall Assessment** | **3.5** (Borderline Conference) | The paper identifies a real and practically important phenomenon (Likert insensitivity to style bias detected by pairwise comparison), demonstrates it with thorough statistical methodology, and provides honest treatment of confounds. However, the small sample sizes, limited judge coverage (2 positive + 1 negative), absence of human evaluation for the NLI gate, and the unresolved construct-validity alternative for the Blindspot framing prevent confident acceptance at the main conference level. With the addition of a third target judge and human NLI validation, this would likely reach 4.0. As submitted, it is a strong Findings candidate or borderline main conference paper. |\n\n**Recommendation**: 3.5 -- Borderline Conference. The statistical methodology is among the most rigorous I have seen at this venue for this type of study, and the Blindspot finding has clear practical value. The main barriers to a higher score are (1) limited generalizability from only two biased judges, (2) the absence of human evaluation for the critical NLI content-preservation claim, and (3) the need for more careful engagement with the construct-validity alternative interpretation. If the authors can address these in revision (particularly items 1 and 2, which are empirical additions rather than conceptual changes), I would expect to raise my score."}],"totalDurationMs":139392,"totalTokens":65044,"totalToolUseCount":18,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2866,"cache_read_input_tokens":58369,"output_tokens":3808,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2866},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":3808,"cache_read_input_tokens":58369,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2866,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2866,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":9,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":6}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"af1a0526cf97c4937","tool_use_id":"toolu_01X9EkUtMXANqBevXG1jbZBV","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":78531,"tool_uses":29,"duration_ms":176697},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"cdca60f2-5ac6-41b5-8fa7-0918a3eddeb5","session_id":"05b18906-f618-42bb-ba5a-27a6b0fbf38c"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01G95n3a7miyZcs366bq9Xm5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016yDLSUoYB3ZyrQFGAYuozo","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"causal_style_decomp_judge","tier":"median","dream_id":"dream_20260521T132932","overall_score":"3.0 — Findings","report":"# R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work Positioning\n\n**Paper**: \"The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Controlled Style Decomposition\"\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\nThis paper introduces CSD (Controlled Style Decomposition), a 3-stage black-box framework for detecting per-axis style bias in LLM judges. The pipeline combines (1) counterfactual style rewriting along isolated axes, (2) NLI-based content gating, and (3) position-controlled pairwise comparison with Bradley-Terry modeling. The central claim is the \"Likert Blindspot\"—that standard Likert scoring systematically attenuates style bias that pairwise comparison readily detects. Experiments span 2 target judges (GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B), 1 negative control (Claude Sonnet 4), 3 benchmarks, and 3 style axes. GEE decomposition reveals a two-tier confound structure (verbosity = pure style bias; formality/register = conditional effects with originality confounds). Seven statistical methods confirm the Likert–pairwise sensitivity gap.\n\n---\n\n## Strengths\n\n### S1: Thorough statistical methodology\nThe paper demonstrates unusually rigorous statistical practice for an NLP submission. The seven-method sensitivity hierarchy (Table 2), Holm-Bonferroni correction for the primary 2x3 family, GEE conditional decomposition with correlation structure robustness checks, Bradley-Terry goodness-of-fit tests, TOST equivalence testing for the negative control, and the explicit distinction between exploratory and confirmatory analyses all reflect genuine statistical literacy. The scaled replication at n=355 that distinguishes measurement attenuation from insufficient power is a particularly convincing piece of evidence.\n\n### S2: Honest treatment of confounds and artifacts\nThe paper is admirably transparent about rewriting artifacts. The double-rewrite placebo reveals that judges prefer LLM-polished text (formality DR win 68-73%, verbosity 94-100%), and rather than hiding this, the authors acknowledge it and use GEE decomposition to separate style from artifact effects. The verbosity Originality OR being non-significant while the style OR is massive (11-13) provides clean evidence. The NLI gate ablation showing formality reversal to 41.7% in NLI-fail pairs is a clever anti-amplification argument.\n\n### S3: Per-axis decomposition reveals meaningful structure\nThe finding that different style axes have qualitatively different confound structures (verbosity = clean style bias, formality = conditional, register = originality-dominated) is genuinely informative. This motivates per-axis analysis over aggregate style metrics and provides actionable guidance for practitioners.\n\n### S4: Comprehensive related work\nThe related work section is well-organized and covers the relevant landscape thoroughly. The positioning against RATE, CASSE, Comparative Trap, and the measurement theory literature (Thurstone 1927, Stevens 1946) is fair. The paper correctly identifies the gap it fills and does not overclaim relative to prior work. The bibliography in the compiled PDF appears intact with 40+ properly formatted references.\n\n### S5: Well-written with clear presentation\nThe paper is generally well-written. Tables are clear and informative. The DAG (Figure 2) effectively communicates the assumed causal structure with appropriate caveats. The forest plot (Figure 3) and power curve (Figure 4) are well-designed. The paper maintains a consistent narrative throughout.\n\n---\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n### W1 (Major): The \"Likert Blindspot\" is a predictable consequence, not a novel finding\nThe paper's central contribution—that pairwise comparison is more sensitive than Likert scoring—is well-established in psychometrics since Thurstone (1927), as the paper itself acknowledges. The authors claim their contribution is \"the first systematic quantification of this gap for LLM judge style bias,\" but this is a narrow instantiation of a known phenomenon. The tie-inflation mechanism (60% of Likert pairs receive identical integer scores) is the expected behavior of coarse integer scales applied to subtle effects—DeCoster et al. (2011), which the paper cites, already establishes this formally. The seven-method sensitivity hierarchy (Table 2) is thorough but fundamentally confirms what measurement theory predicts: forced-choice detects smaller effects than absolute rating. The \"Likert Blindspot\" branding attempts to elevate a predictable measurement artifact into a discovery. A fairer framing would be: \"we provide empirical confirmation, in the LLM-judge setting, of the well-known measurement theory prediction that pairwise comparison has higher sensitivity than absolute rating scales.\"\n\n**Severity**: This substantially weakens the novelty of C2 (the paper's stated \"main contribution\" per the framing document). The finding is real but incremental—it is domain transfer of established psychometric knowledge, not a new insight about measurement.\n\n### W2 (Major): Only 3 judges (2 biased + 1 not) is insufficient for \"architecture-dependent\" claims\nThe paper tests GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B, and Claude Sonnet 4. Both target judges show bias; the negative control does not. With n=3 judges, the paper cannot credibly claim that bias is \"architecture-dependent\" in any meaningful sense—this is just \"some judges are biased, some aren't.\" The paper's own framing document acknowledges Llama-3.3-70B was pending but apparently did not make it into the submitted version. The distilled research skill notes that the field standard is 3-5 heterogeneous judges. Three judges (two of which show similar bias patterns) provides almost no statistical basis for claims about architecture-dependence. The title in the task description mentions \"Architecture-Dependent Style Bias\" but the submitted paper wisely avoids this in its title—the data simply cannot support such a claim.\n\n**Recommendation**: Either add 2+ more judges (Gemini, Llama, Mistral) or significantly downscope the architecture-dependence claims to \"we observe variation across the three judges tested.\"\n\n### W3 (Major): The Comparative Trap concern is not fully resolved\nJeong et al. (2025) show that pairwise comparison amplifies style biases relative to pointwise scoring. The paper presents four counter-arguments (NLI ablation, Claude negative control, cross-axis gradient, debiased prompting). However:\n\n- The NLI ablation (n≈12 for NLI-fail) has very limited statistical power and the authors acknowledge this is \"a bounding analysis\"\n- The Claude negative control proves Claude is not biased, but does not prove GPT-4o/Qwen3 bias is not format-amplified\n- The cross-axis gradient is a qualitative 3-point correlation (register < formality < verbosity), which provides weak evidence\n- The paper's own Limitations section concedes it \"cannot fully exclude format-level amplification without human ground-truth ratings\"\n\nThe paper honestly acknowledges this limitation, but it remains the fundamental identifiability challenge: without human ground truth on the same counterfactual pairs, we cannot separate \"CSD detects real bias\" from \"CSD detects real bias + amplifies it.\" The practical implication may be identical (as the paper argues), but the scientific claim is weaker.\n\n### W4 (Moderate): Evaluator-producer bias is inadequately controlled\nQwen3-32B serves as both rewriter and judge in the primary experiments. The rewriter independence test (Appendix D) shows the Style OR decreases by 22% (13.10 → 10.17) when GPT-4o is substituted as rewriter, which is a non-trivial reduction. The paper argues this \"bounds\" self-preference inflation, but a 22% reduction in the primary effect size is substantial enough to warrant GPT-4o-rewritten data as the primary analysis (or at least co-primary), not just a robustness check. For formality, the BT difference is smaller (64.4% vs 66.5%), which is reassuring, but the verbosity reduction is concerning. Register results, which the paper acknowledges carry the highest evaluator-producer risk, lack any rewriter independence validation.\n\n### W5 (Moderate): Single primary benchmark (LLMBar, n=100)\nAll primary analyses use LLMBar with n=100 instruction-response pairs. Cross-benchmark validation on AlpacaEval and MT-Bench is limited to formality (and AlpacaEval verbosity for Qwen3 only). The n=88 effective sample size after NLI filtering means many analyses are underpowered for detecting moderate effects. The scaled replication at n=355 for one configuration (Qwen3 formality) is valuable but isolated. Whether these findings generalize beyond short-form instruction-following tasks is an open question.\n\n### W6 (Minor): Novelty of the CSD framework itself\nCSD combines three existing techniques: (1) LLM-based counterfactual rewriting (used in RATE, Counterfactual Rhetorical), (2) NLI-based content verification (standard in NLI literature), and (3) position-controlled pairwise comparison + BT modeling (standard in Chatbot Arena, AlpacaEval). The combination is competent engineering but each component is individually well-established. The per-axis decomposition adds structure, and the GEE confound analysis adds analytical depth, but the framework contribution is primarily integrative. The paper positions this honestly (\"no prior work combines all three\") but this \"first to combine X+Y+Z\" framing is thin novelty.\n\n### W7 (Minor): Missing ablation on scale granularity\nThe paper identifies tie inflation in 1-10 integer scales as the Blindspot mechanism but does not test whether finer-grained scales (e.g., continuous 0-100, or 0.5-increment) mitigate the problem. This is acknowledged in Limitations (\"whether finer-grained scales could mitigate the Blindspot remains open\") but since the entire paper hinges on this mechanism, even a small-scale ablation would be informative.\n\n---\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n1. **On the Comparative Trap**: If you had human annotations on a subset of your counterfactual pairs (e.g., 50 pairs judged by 3 humans each), could you distinguish detection from amplification? Would you consider this a necessary experiment for the camera-ready?\n\n2. **On architecture-dependence**: With only 3 judges, what specific claim are you making about architecture? Is it simply \"judges differ\" or do you claim to identify which architectural features predict bias? If the former, is this surprising?\n\n3. **On scale granularity**: Have you considered testing the same counterfactual pairs with a continuous scale (e.g., \"rate on a scale from 0 to 100\") to determine whether the Blindspot is specific to coarse integer scales or generalizes to all absolute rating formats?\n\n4. **On the GEE decomposition**: The interaction test (p=0.53 for GPT-4o, p=0.32 for Qwen3-32B) suggests additive effects, but with n=88, you have very limited power to detect interactions. Can you report the interaction effect size to assess whether absence of evidence is evidence of absence here?\n\n5. **On verbosity as \"pure style bias\"**: The Originality OR is 0.49 (p=.096) for Qwen3 and 0.45 (p=.062) for GPT-4o. These are trending toward significance and the ORs suggest the original is actually preferred over the rewrite after controlling for style—which could indicate a residual artifact. How confident are you that this is truly \"no observed originality confound\" rather than an underpowered test that would reach significance at n=200?\n\n6. **On the rewriting artifact**: The double-rewrite placebo shows near-zero original win rate for verbosity (GPT-4o 6.2%, Qwen3 0%). This means judges overwhelmingly prefer the rewrite for reasons unrelated to the target style axis. How does this affect the interpretation of the verbosity Style OR, given that the GEE decomposition may not fully separate these effects?\n\n---\n\n## Missing References\n\nThe bibliography is intact in the compiled PDF (references properly formatted on pages 11-13). However, the following relevant works appear to be missing:\n\n1. **Saito et al. (2023)** \"Verbosity Bias in Multi-turn Chatbot Evaluations\" — directly relevant to the verbosity findings\n2. **Shi et al. (2024)** \"Judging LLM-as-a-Judge with MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena\" follow-up work on judge calibration\n3. **Huang et al. (2024)** \"Bias Benchmark for Large Language Models\" — systematic bias taxonomy\n4. **Chen et al. (2024)** \"Humans or LLMs as the Judge? A Study on Judgement Biases\" — directly compares human and LLM judge biases, relevant to the format-amplification question\n5. The paper cites Koo et al. (2024) under the key `koo2024cobbler` but the title in the bib is \"Benchmarking Cognitive Biases in Large Language Models as Evaluators\" — this is the CoBBLEr paper and is correctly cited, but the task description mentions \"Koo et al. 2023\" which suggests there may be confusion about the publication year\n\n---\n\n## Scores\n\n| Criterion | Score | Justification |\n|-----------|-------|---------------|\n| **Excitement** | 2.5 / 5 | The core finding (pairwise > Likert for detecting style bias) is predictable from measurement theory. The per-axis confound decomposition is the most interesting contribution but is presented as secondary. The \"Likert Blindspot\" branding overclaims novelty. |\n| **Soundness** | 3.5 / 5 | Statistical methodology is strong—among the best I have seen in NLP submissions. However, the Comparative Trap concern (detection vs. amplification) is not fully resolved, evaluator-producer bias is inadequately controlled, and the judge sample is too small for architecture-dependence claims. |\n| **Clarity** | 4.0 / 5 | Well-written, clear tables and figures, honest limitations section. The related work is comprehensive. Minor issue: the paper is dense and the appendix is substantial (5 pages), suggesting borderline page-budget management. |\n| **Significance** | 2.5 / 5 | The practical recommendation (use pairwise comparison for style bias audits) is sound but unsurprising. The per-axis decomposition framework has modest utility. The finding that \"some judges are biased, some aren't\" is expected given prior literature. |\n| **Overall Assessment** | **3.0** | Findings-level paper. Strong statistical methodology and honest artifact handling elevate the execution quality, but the novelty ceiling is low (domain transfer of known psychometric phenomena) and the empirical scope is narrow (3 judges, 1 primary benchmark). The Likert Blindspot, while real, is a predictable consequence rather than a discovery. The per-axis confound decomposition is the most novel element but is underdeveloped as a contribution. Suitable for Findings given execution quality; insufficient novelty and scope for Main. |\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Calibration Notes\n\n**Why not 3.5 (Borderline Conference)**: The novelty ceiling is structural. The central contribution is applying Thurstone (1927) to LLM judges—a valid but incremental domain transfer. The judge sample (n=3) is below the field standard (3-5 heterogeneous judges per the distilled research skill). The Comparative Trap remains unresolved. For Main acceptance, I would need either (a) a genuinely surprising finding (e.g., that some architectural feature predicts bias, with enough judges to test this), or (b) a correction mechanism that demonstrably improves downstream evaluation quality—not just detection.\n\n**Why not 2.5 (Borderline Findings)**: The statistical methodology is genuinely excellent. The GEE confound decomposition reveals meaningful structure. The rewriting artifact handling is among the most honest I have seen. The paper correctly identifies a real problem (Likert insensitivity to style bias) even if the finding is predictable. The related work is comprehensive and the positioning is fair. These execution qualities warrant Findings.\n\n**Path to 4.0**: (1) Add 2+ more judges to credibly claim architecture-dependence, (2) include human evaluation on a subset to bound format-level amplification, (3) test finer-grained scales to isolate the tie-inflation mechanism, (4) promote the per-axis confound decomposition as the primary novelty rather than the Likert Blindspot.\n","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":750,"cache_read_input_tokens":77017,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":750,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01X9EkUtMXANqBevXG1jbZBV","session_id":"05b18906-f618-42bb-ba5a-27a6b0fbf38c","uuid":"6936868a-13b7-4c86-9def-a2b8273e4e80"}
