{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01X9EkUtMXANqBevXG1jbZBV","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## R2 Review Summary\n\nThe review has been submitted. Here are the key findings:\n\n**Overall Assessment: 3.0 (Findings)**\n\n**Core tension**: The paper executes well on a low-novelty premise. The \"Likert Blindspot\" is a valid empirical observation but a predictable consequence of Thurstone (1927) — pairwise comparison has been known to be more sensitive than absolute rating for nearly a century. The paper's framing elevates this domain transfer into a \"discovery,\" which overclaims.\n\n**Major weaknesses (3)**:\n1. **Low novelty ceiling** — The Likert Blindspot is the expected behavior of coarse integer scales applied to subtle effects, confirmed by the paper's own measurement theory citations (DeCoster 2011, Stevens 1946, Thurstone 1927).\n2. **Insufficient judge coverage** — Only 3 judges (2 biased, 1 not) cannot support architecture-dependence claims. Field standard is 3-5 heterogeneous judges.\n3. **Comparative Trap unresolved** — The paper cannot separate detection from format-level amplification without human ground truth. The four counter-arguments are individually weak (NLI ablation n≈12, Claude control only proves Claude is unbiased, cross-axis gradient is 3-point qualitative).\n\n**Key strengths**:\n- Exceptionally rigorous statistical methodology (Holm-Bonferroni, GEE, TOST, BT GoF, seven-method hierarchy)\n- Honest artifact handling — double-rewrite placebo acknowledged and controlled via GEE\n- Per-axis confound decomposition reveals genuinely informative structure (verbosity = pure, formality = conditional, register = originality-dominated)\n- Comprehensive related work with proper positioning\n\n**Path to 4.0**: Add more judges, include human evaluation to bound amplification, test finer-grained scales, and reframe the per-axis confound decomposition as the primary novelty rather than the Likert Blindspot."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: af1a0526cf97c4937 (use SendMessage with to: 'af1a0526cf97c4937' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 82545\ntool_uses: 29\nduration_ms: 192109</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"05b18906-f618-42bb-ba5a-27a6b0fbf38c","uuid":"b8183dff-401c-4926-99b2-07c41ecf0d6c","timestamp":"2026-05-21T14:28:19.553Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 ARR submission. Your focus is **novelty and related work positioning**.\n\n## Venue Calibration\nEMNLP 2026 ARR Overall Assessment scale (1-5, half-point):\n- 5 = Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Conference acceptance\n- 3 = Findings acceptance\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\nAcceptance rate ~21%. Be calibrated accordingly.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"Detecting Architecture-Dependent Style Bias in LLM Judges via Controlled Style Decomposition\"\n\nThe paper introduces CSD (Controlled Style Decomposition), a 3-stage black-box framework for per-judge, per-axis style bias detection in LLM judges:\n1. Counterfactual generation: style-manipulated pairs along isolated axes (formality, verbosity, register)\n2. NLI-based content gating: bidirectional entailment filtering\n3. Position-controlled pairwise evaluation + Bradley-Terry modeling\n\nKey findings:\n- Architecture-dependent bias: 2/4 judges (GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B) show significant style bias; 2/4 (Claude, Llama) show none\n- \"Likert Blindspot\": 83% of biased cells detected only by pairwise comparison\n- Amplification is architecture-dependent (8-11pp biased judges, 2-4pp unbiased)\n- Conservative corrected estimates throughout\n- Cross-benchmark consistency, rewriter independence\n\n## Related Work as presented in paper (§2):\nThree paragraphs:\n1. \"Style bias in LLM evaluation\" — cites SOSBench, unnamed verbose/formal preference studies. Claims prior work diagnoses at benchmark level, not individual judge level, and none provide per-axis decomposition with content control.\n2. \"Pairwise vs. Likert evaluation\" — cites Jeong et al. \"Comparative Trap.\" Claims complementary finding: amplification is architecture-dependent.\n3. \"Content-controlled evaluation\" — cites RATE (ATE for style bias but Likert-based), CASSE (multi-dimensional but correlational), LC-AlpacaEval (single dimension). Claims CSD is first to combine NLI-gated content control + per-axis decomposition + pairwise comparison.\n\n**NOTE**: All citations in the PDF appear as \"(?)\" or \"(??)\" — meaning the bibliography is missing or broken. This is a significant formatting issue.\n\n## Contributions claimed:\nC1: CSD framework (methodology) — first per-judge, per-axis bias profiling tool\nC2: Architecture-dependent bias finding — 2/4 split, judge-specific not format-universal\nC3: Likert Blindspot formalization — 83% of biased cells missed by Likert\nC4: Honest amplification accounting — placebo-controlled, conservative correction\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed R2 review focusing on:\n\n1. **Novelty assessment**: How novel is CSD compared to RATE, CASSE, Comparative Trap? Is the combination of existing techniques sufficient for a novelty claim? Is \"architecture-dependent bias\" a genuinely new finding?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are important baselines or prior works missing? Is the positioning fair?\n3. **Contribution significance**: Are the claims appropriately scoped? Is the 2/4 split sufficient to claim \"architecture-dependent\"? \n4. **Narrative coherence**: Does the story hold together? Is \"Likert Blindspot\" well-motivated?\n5. **Writing quality and presentation**: Is the paper well-written? Tables clear? Any formatting issues?\n6. **Missing citations issue**: The bibliography appears entirely broken (all \"?\" references)\n\nConsider these specific questions:\n- Is combining NLI gating + pairwise + per-axis decomposition genuinely novel, or is it incremental engineering?\n- With only 4 judges (2 biased, 2 not), can the paper credibly claim \"architecture-dependent\" patterns? Or is this just \"some judges are biased, some aren't\"?\n- The \"Likert Blindspot\" framing — is this a genuine measurement insight or a predictable consequence of Likert's known lower sensitivity?\n- How does this compare to concurrent work on style bias (e.g., Koo et al. 2023 \"Benchmarking Cognitive Biases in LLM-as-Judge\", Wu et al. 2024 \"Style Over Substance\")?\n\nProvide scores for:\n- Excitement (1-5) — how novel/impactful is this?\n- Soundness (1-5) — from a framing/claims perspective\n- Overall Assessment (1-5, half-point, calibrated to EMNLP acceptance rate)\n\nFormat your review as a structured EMNLP-style review with: Summary, Strengths, Weaknesses, Questions for Authors, Missing References, and Scores.","agentId":"af1a0526cf97c4937","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## R2 Review Summary\n\nThe review has been submitted. Here are the key findings:\n\n**Overall Assessment: 3.0 (Findings)**\n\n**Core tension**: The paper executes well on a low-novelty premise. The \"Likert Blindspot\" is a valid empirical observation but a predictable consequence of Thurstone (1927) — pairwise comparison has been known to be more sensitive than absolute rating for nearly a century. The paper's framing elevates this domain transfer into a \"discovery,\" which overclaims.\n\n**Major weaknesses (3)**:\n1. **Low novelty ceiling** — The Likert Blindspot is the expected behavior of coarse integer scales applied to subtle effects, confirmed by the paper's own measurement theory citations (DeCoster 2011, Stevens 1946, Thurstone 1927).\n2. **Insufficient judge coverage** — Only 3 judges (2 biased, 1 not) cannot support architecture-dependence claims. Field standard is 3-5 heterogeneous judges.\n3. **Comparative Trap unresolved** — The paper cannot separate detection from format-level amplification without human ground truth. The four counter-arguments are individually weak (NLI ablation n≈12, Claude control only proves Claude is unbiased, cross-axis gradient is 3-point qualitative).\n\n**Key strengths**:\n- Exceptionally rigorous statistical methodology (Holm-Bonferroni, GEE, TOST, BT GoF, seven-method hierarchy)\n- Honest artifact handling — double-rewrite placebo acknowledged and controlled via GEE\n- Per-axis confound decomposition reveals genuinely informative structure (verbosity = pure, formality = conditional, register = originality-dominated)\n- Comprehensive related work with proper positioning\n\n**Path to 4.0**: Add more judges, include human evaluation to bound amplification, test finer-grained scales, and reframe the per-axis confound decomposition as the primary novelty rather than the Likert Blindspot."}],"totalDurationMs":192109,"totalTokens":82545,"totalToolUseCount":29,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4307,"cache_read_input_tokens":77767,"output_tokens":470,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4307},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":470,"cache_read_input_tokens":77767,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4307,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4307,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":15,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":11}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013rkpMLdhyWEbbU5RorbmPC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good, I have both reviews. Let me now get the full R2 review by asking the agent for the complete review text, since it seems to have returned a summary. Actually, looking at both outputs, R1 gave a very detailed review, and R2 gave a summary. Let me ask R2 for the full detailed review.\n\nActually, looking more carefully, R2's output seems to be a summary of its findings. The full review was likely generated internally. Let me proceed with the roundtable synthesis based on what I have from both reviewers.\n\nLet me now conduct the full roundtable:\n- Step 1: Independent reviews (done - R1 and R2 have submitted)\n\n\n- Step 2: Cross-sharing - identify agreements and disagreements\n- Step 3: Convergence - resolve disagreements\n- Step 4: Synthesize final report\n\nNow I'll move forward with the cross-sharing phase to map out where the reviewers align and where they diverge, then work through resolving any conflicts before producing the final synthesis.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7838,"cache_read_input_tokens":56073,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7838},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"05b18906-f618-42bb-ba5a-27a6b0fbf38c","uuid":"ebd9f0e3-39e8-4e3d-b922-07ee614151e6"}
